{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1561664-protean-a-rant-and-some-homebrewed-solutions-looking-for-opinions","id":1561664,"title":"Protean - A Rant and some Homebrewed solutions? Looking for Opinions","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2026-07-13T16:15:45-07:00","id":1561664,"title":"Protean - A Rant and some Homebrewed solutions? Looking for Opinions","content":"<span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Hello all!</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Today I want to rant about my least favorite Discipline: Protean.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">But first, let's talk about what I like about the other Disciplines.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">In Requiem, most Disciplines kind of build upon the base layer as they go up. Auspex, Dominate, Majesty, Nightmare, and Obfuscate all feel like they progress naturally. Each level beyond the first feels like an improvement and an expansion of what it can do.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Animalism is a bit weird, which happens to be my second least favorite Discipline. Levels 1 to 3 feel natural, then you get into Levels 4 and 5, which are cool on their own, but feel disconnected from the previous three levels. Anyway, Animalism doesn't cause me as much issue as Protean does, so I'll focus on that.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I have two main issues: a thematic one and a mechanical one (with Protean 3).</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Thematic Issue</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Protean progression doesn\u2019t feel natural at all and is all over the place. I get it, Protean is the \u2018\u2019Mythological power we couldn\u2019t fit into another discipline\u2019\u2019 kind of discipline, but still.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">When you first learn the discipline, you learn to merge with soil, brick, or even concrete. How? why? It mentions that it creates a cocoon of soil around the user, but how does it work in a 5-inch brick wall? Or in the floor? One has to assume the vampire kinda melds somewhere. It\u2019s, magical, I guess.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Now the Second dot is cool, it\u2019s IMO, what Protean is truly about; you learn to manifest animal traits. Very cool, but how you got there from earth melding is \u2026 certainly a stretch.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Third dot and fourth dot make sense coming from the 2<sup>nd</sup> dot. Transforming into an animal after having learned the ability to manifest some of their trait, is both cool and make sense. The Fourth dot expands on the 2<sup>nd</sup> dot, which I also like.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Then there\u2019s the fifth dots; You become mist. Okay, what\u2019s the link with all the rest? Not much to be honest. It has\u2026some very minor connection to unmarked grave, if you ignore the fluff about creating a cocoon and imaging the vampire melding, but anyway.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Mechanical Issues</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I have a few mechanical issues with some of the powers.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Unmarked Grave:</b> It's not interesting; it's flat-out boring. All other Disciplines have a cool one-dot ability.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Physical Disciplines have useful activations as soon as the first dot is invested. Celerity 1 is awesome on its own and allows you to Clash of Wills against other uses of Celerity, which you flat-out can't do if you don't have one dot. Vigor 1 doubles your jump distance for free, which is pretty cool. Resilience is the least glamorous, but for one dot, two points of activatable armor can really save you from torpor or even staking.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Even Nightmare, which I find kind of meh, still has some fun minor illusion effects you can pull off at one dot.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">With Protean 1, your power makes you better at sleeping. Yay.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Beast's Skin:</b> Protean 3 is the one I have the most mechanical gripes with. Since the user gains all of the animal's physical Attributes and Skills, it encourages Gangrel, a physical Clan, not to invest in physical Skills at all.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Why build Stealth when you can shapeshift into a rat or a cat and have a pool of 8?</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Why build Survival when you can turn into a wolf and gain 4 dots?</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Why invest anything into combat when you can turn into a bear and have a grappling pool of 10 and a Health pool of 13 before any Vigor or Resilience is factored in?</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">You might say, &quot;Well, a cat isn't powerful enough for a surprise attack, and a bear isn't stealthy at all.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Might I present you with the best of both worlds: the great cat!</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">With a base Stealth pool of 7 and a fighting pool of 9 or 10 (with claws), it's only one shy of being as effective as a maxed-out fighter like the bear, but it also sports much better Defense at a base Defense of 8, before Celerity. Its main drawback is that it only has 8 Health boxes, but that's nothing Resilience, which happens to be a Clan Discipline, can't fix.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Basically, you are discouraged from investing in any Physical Attribute, as well as Athletics, Brawl, Survival, and Stealth because not only do you gain access to all the animal's physical traits, but you also lose access to your own.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Unnatural Aspect:</b> I simply dislike Barbed Hands. Grappling is already strong; it doesn't need extra incentive. If you want to be the best at grappling, invest in Vigor, I don't know. It's yet another thing that stacks in favor of grappling, which combat almost always devolves into, in my experience.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Primeval Miasma:</b> I've read that since Kindred don't breathe, you can't steal Vitae from inside their lungs. If Strix can't steal breath from Kindred, this power shouldn't be able to do it either. If you can use it to steal Vitae from Kindred while inside them, I hate it. Otherwise, I don't have any mechanical issues with it, outside of the fact that it might be a bit of an auto-win against mortals, but to be fair, mortals never stood a chance anyway. Certainly more fair than Swarm form (which I also disallow).</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Solutions</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">All right, I'm done ranting. I'm looking at solutions. Here's what I'm thinking.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Unmarked Grave:</b> Unmarked Grave becomes a Devotion using Protean and Resilience, probably one dot of each, as the power isn't that interesting on its own.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">If Unmarked grave is a devotion, Protean 1 becomes a minor version of Predatory Aspect that costs 0 Vitae but allows you to choose two adaptations, plus one per dot of Protean after the first. You can only manifest one at a time with a reflexive action. If you use Protean 2, you can manifest all of them at once.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Beast's Skin:</b> Transforming into an animal gives. You gain a number of transformations points equal to your protean + 3. Each points allows you to buy one of the following:</span><ul><li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Increase Strength, Dexterity, Stamina or Wits by 1, up to Blood Potency Limit.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Increase size by 1, up to Protean + 3. </span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Increase speed by 2. </span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Increase : Athletics, Brawl, Intimidation, Stealth and Survival, up to Blood Potency Limit.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Your form has access to Flight or Swim. (Free if you already have access to aquatic(swim) or patagia (flight). </span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Reduce your size by any amount. </span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Gain a natural weapon (Free if you already have access to claws with Predatory aspect.)</span></li> </ul><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Those forms are fixed, and you can have up to protean form stored. You can change them as usual and they should represent predators.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I don\u2019t know if it\u2019s too weak, but that way I feel like\u2026 It grants the versatility of having multiple animal shapes, while not completely discouraging investment into physical stats. If you want your Strength 1, Brawl 0 nerd to be good in combat, it can certainly increase your combat capability by +6 (By adding +3 STR and +3 brawl) at protean 3, which is very good, but it won\u2019t turn you into a combat god either. If you\u2019re already good, it might push you a bit over your limit, to truly make you scary, but it won\u2019t make it mandatory for any fighter to invest too protean.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Barbed Hands</b>: I'm not sure whether I want to remove it or simply state that it doesn't stack with Vigor. Maybe it could add Protean as grapple damage on a successful grapple instead of adding to the grapple roll. Still useful, less mandatory.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Primeval Miasma:</b></span><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"> I don't truly care, to be honest. Five-dot Disciplines are rare in most of my campaigns. Maybe it can stay. It makes little thematic sense with the rest of the Protean, but at that point, the vampire can already shapeshift into an amphibious, echolocating, wall-crawling bear with wings, so maybe turning into smoke isn't that far-fetched after all... Beside, mythological power, yada yada. </span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I don't know what an appropriate power would be otherwise. Maybe a lesser version of the Force of Nature Devotion. Primeval Miasma could become a Protean 4 + Obfuscate 3 Devotion.</span><br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Conclusion</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">That was a long rant. Hopefully not too negative.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Anyone had the similar issues? Are you already fine with Protean?</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Any critics about my half-assed solutions?</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Any other solutions to propose?</span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26771,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","user":"Algarik","date":". 07-13-2026, 07:56 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-13T18:35:52-07:00","id":1561670,"title":"","content":"2e's version of Protean 1 represents the culmination of the ability to merge with the earth moving forward in the Discipline's powerset from Masquerade to Requiem 1e to Requiem 2e, and it makes this the thematic base of the ladder by making it the means of refitting the kit provided by the middle dots (remaining a cheaper means of doing so than Primeval Miasma in most situations).<br /> <br /> Blood and Smoke made it a specific design point that the first dot of a Discipline is its gateway to monstrosity, with later powers ratcheting up the intensity. Unmarked Grave serves as the introduction to the vampiric ability to transform their bodies by allowing them to dispense with the specifics of their material form on one hand and become a preternaturally buried corpse on the other hand.<br /> <br /> This being the introductory power of the Gangrel's proprietary Discipline makes it very easy to explain how they maintain their reputation as nomads and wild folk around population centers despite a propensity for behavior that may put them on the outs in the All Night Society. It gives them a reason to keep soil and lower-Durability chunks of material handy even if they have a dedicated Haven.<br /> <br /> Yes, visually, manifesting animal traits is the classic power of Protean. Thematically, though, it's about manifesting the Beast, and literally every published version of Protean in both Vampire games so far puts concrete mobile shapechanging above the range of the first dot, and definitely doesn't fit the general 2e design space of starter powers being mostly free. &quot;You are swallowed by the earth and can drink blood spilled upon it in comparative safety or stir the cauldron of your Beast's stronger manifestations&quot; is a decent complementary ability to the kit of &quot;be harder to damage&quot; or &quot;talk to an animal and give it simple commands.&quot;<br /> <br /> <br /> The raised issues with the Trait replacements of Beast's Skin cheerfully ignore the simple ludonarrative facts that this is a game where you are trying to maintain your Humanity, shapeshifting costs more Vitae than a starting character can spend in one turn when you need Vitae to be active, and things that aren't humanlike actors are going to give you less Vitae while humanlike actors are unlikely to react positively to the type of animals you can turn into, to say nothing of the loss of opposable thumbs and ability to speak. (And, not to put too fine a point on it, needing to find and kill a given animal first to add it to your limited library of shapes.)<br /> <br /> Mild similarity of mechanical framework notwithstanding, the Kindred are not mages and do not benefit tremendously from pursuing magical development over personal and political advancement. Other Disciplines have a hard limit on their ability to supplement the fixed traits of any given animal and animal forms have a limited ability to overcome, say, an elder who's spent decades honing their skill in unarmed combat to superhuman levels without needing to waste precious blood turning into a bear first.<br /> <br /> <br /> Speaking of comparing to mages, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1420717-game-design-disciplines-how-and-why\">I had a thread some years ago</a> asking about why Disciplines are so structures when the Blood is so variable and reflecting on that means my reaction to your proposed alternatives to Protean 1 and Protean 3 is a firm &quot;Nah.&quot; The language has shifted away from the more &quot;lifeless&quot; manifestations of it over the years, but it remains a fact that vampires aren't vital \u2014 their powers are accumulated tendencies of the Beast in the blood, and that means the closer you get to fully freeform shapeshifting on the fly as a universal quality of the Discipline the further you're getting from individual vampires as (to borrow neutral language from a different subtopic) creatures of theme. Unmarked Grave and Primeval Miasma allowing a vampire to dream up a new Beastly loadout a day later is appropriately metered for undead shapeshifters without the roiling flesh of werewolves or the chaotic elemental sorcery of mages.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-14T04:25:22-07:00","id":1561675,"title":"","content":"I'm under the impression the issue is more table culture than the Discipline. Which isn't a problem necessarily, it just means the playstyle of the group isn't like most.<br /> <br /> Protean is not about animal shape change. It has never been. It's about the whole range of physical transmutation of the undead body and being adaptive. So, animal forms aren't a basis of it, they're just one more application.<br /> <br /> Unmarked Grave is a must-have to live in the wilds or otherwise as an undead survivalist. It's cool as a theme of a monster that haunts the very land. I don't just find it fitting, but hard to give up on.<br /> <br /> Other problem is that other Disciplines make a point of having all levels being useful. Not just build upon the theme, but they either add effect or alternative uses rather than just being &quot;last, but better&quot;. Your proposal extends a kit that is already in the limit of thematic stretch and go straight into redundancy.<br /> <br /> For the animal forms, between the need to get and defeat the animal, giving up on equipment and generally being animal with all that entails, I don't see a big problem with the traits gained. It's costly and constrained in many ways.<br /> <br /> Barbed Hands don't strike me as a problem if the character really wants to go the whole nine yards and pay the many associated costs, but if your group really goes Grapple all the time it's fine to get it out, I guess? Maybe examining the reason why this is happening may be relevant, but if devolving into grappling every time is fun for you and your group, no reason to worry.<br /> <br /> So, similar issues? Can't say I have. Issues with your proposals? I think they're kind of flattening the Discipline. Other solutions? I think if you do want to stick to animal forms all the way, you need to find other ways to use them rather than just make everything build-your-form. Crucially, question each level which problem it must solve that others don't, starting with how to get cover from the sun in the run.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-14T13:55:07-07:00","id":1561694,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Both interesting answers that go in a similar direction, so I'll answer each point I can make out in general.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Gaming Style</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">My issues with Protean are definitely table culture issues.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I tend to run Requiem as a sandbox-style political drama. Currently, I run my second chronicle in my homebrewed version of Tokyo.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">My game runs on a calendar system I made to track time. Basically, the game runs on a monthly basis, with four weeks plus one weekend representing Elysium night. Each week, players get:</span><ul><li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">1 feeding action to reset their Hunger dice, representing how well they hunt during the week. (I homebrewed Hunger dice into the system.)</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">1 personal action they can devote to... anything, really. It can be a Touchstone scene to regain Willpower, they can work on a plot kit, find rumors, etc. They must make a Rouse check before this action to see if they get hungrier.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">1 coterie action they must use in conjunction with their coterie to advance whatever the coterie wants to work on. They must also make a Rouse check before their action to see if they get hungrier.</span></li> </ul><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">At the end of the month, Elysium happens, and since it's basically a big convention where a lot of Kindred meet, it's useful for making connections and sharing information without having to spend one of your precious actions on a meeting that could have been resolved with a five-minute conversation at Elysium.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">If someone does not want to participate in a coterie action, they can opt out to regain 1 Willpower by staying home and resting, but they don't get any extra scenes. (This is to avoid &quot;spreading the party&quot; too much and to keep the story moving.)</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Finally, I try to give each player at least one personal scene per month\u2014something that happens specifically to their character. Sometimes more, during busier months.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I make some room for personal horror, and Humanity has its place, but it's definitely not my sole focus. My experience with Humanity is that characters who aren't fighters tend to plateau around Humanity 5, while fighters usually hover around Humanity 3 or 4. Personally, I tend to like making characters who try to act morally and care about the consequences of their actions (which isn't quite the same thing as Humanity), but not all of my players care about that, so I try not to focus on it too much.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Feeding scenes and Touchstone scenes can happen once in a while if I feel like I have a good idea for one, but I find that they tend to become repetitive. I usually reserve Touchstone scenes for when players ask for them. Players\u2014not characters\u2014generally have control over whether they risk their Touchstones.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Combat, as weird as it sounds in a post ranting about Protean, is really not my focus either. It tends to be a last resort or plot-driven. Combat generally happens because the players want it to happen, as those who want to avoid it usually have the tools to do so.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">In my current chronicle, it has happened maybe three times over the course of twenty-one six-hour sessions.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">In the previous chronicle, combat happened a bit more frequently as a result of having an actual fighter, but even then, no more than once every three or four sessions.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Physical characters in my chronicles usually have political weight in the sense that people who know they're dangerous are careful about how they approach or cross them.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Anyway, I'll stop myself here. I mostly wanted to share some insight into how I run my games.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Protean Theme &amp; Discipline Progression</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">My issue here is that Protean, and to some extent Animalism, are the only two Disciplines that stick out like sore thumbs among all the others. Every other Discipline increases in a way that feels like a natural progression from the previous dot. Protean doesn't. Clearly, that's not an issue everyone has, but I tend to prefer when things are a bit more organized.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Might just be a me problem.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Satchel, from what I understand from your other post, it seems like you'd go in a different direction regarding how Disciplines manifest themselves. I'm not sure I completely follow, but I feel like we have opposing views, which is perfectly fine.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I'll also add that any references to other game lines have a high chance of flying over my head, as I haven't played any of them.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I understand that Protean is not the &quot;druidic shapeshifting&quot; Discipline. Even if most of the Discipline is focused on manifesting animal traits, I don't see those traits as natural. They morph the user's body in ways that are unnatural and creepy, and I have no problem with that.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">The description of the Discipline mentions that it's about &quot;[indulging] their Beast, reveling in the descent from an undead thing yearning for its grave to a force of savage power.&quot; Or, as Satchel aptly pointed out, a descent into monstrosity. But even in the book's own description, I see a narrative stretch: it's both &quot;yearning for your grave&quot; and &quot;savage power.&quot; The &quot;yearning for your grave&quot; part only really comes into play with the first dot. The rest has very little to do with it, outside of the fact that future adaptations must be changed with <i>Unmarked Grave</i>. But if <i>Unmarked Grave</i> wasn't part of the Discipline, it wouldn't even be mentioned, and torpor would probably be the default way to change those adaptations.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Which brings me back to my point: Protean, as a Discipline, feels like a collection of otherwise unrelated vampiric mythical powers linked together by the vague idea that they're all tied to shapeshifting.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Why must every Protean user learn how to Earth Meld before they can manifest their bestial traits? Why does the first power of the Discipline have very little to do with manifesting your Beast and everything to do with hiding away?</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I just don't see it.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">It's most certainly a me problem.</span><br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Unmarked Grave</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><i>Unmarked Grave</i> is certainly useful to have. It's an iconic Gangrel power that has carried over from multiple game lines.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">It can be used in a political campaign as an additional intimidation tool. After all, do you really want to enter a turf war with Gangrel when they can sleep anywhere? That's definitely a scary prospect.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">But that's about the extent of what the first dot offers: a backup plan and a safer nomadic lifestyle. If you really want to stalk the wilderness, there's always the possibility of carrying an opaque tent or something. Besides, not all Gangrel are going to be constantly on the move.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">This is why I'm proposing to make it a Protean 1 / Resilience 1 Devotion. It gives easy access to Gangrel who want to play nomads, but it doesn't force the Discipline on all Gangrel\u2014or on any bloodline with access to Protean, for that matter.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I'm aware that all my previous points are subjective, but the next one definitely is, so take it with a grain of salt:</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">To me, <i>Unmarked Grave</i> commits the cardinal sin of being an incredibly boring first-dot Discipline power. It's up there with Resilience as something that only comes up once in a blue moon.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I get that the genre of <i>Vampire: the Requiem</i> leans heavily into the &quot;Woe is me, I'm a cursed supernatural being, vampirism <i>sucks</i>&quot; theme, but nearly every other Discipline offers something awesome right from its very beginning, while Protean 1 doesn't. Let's look at those Disciplines:</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Animalism.</b> You gain access to a whole new realm of possibilities. You can interact with animals in ways that even a mundane expert could never hope to. Incredibly useful, and it costs no Vitae.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Auspex.</b> Incredibly useful. It gives you valuable information at very little risk. It's also one of the few defenses against Obfuscate. The first use is free.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Dominate.</b> Probably the strongest first-dot Discipline. It lets you control someone else and even erase memories of a scene or implant simple ones. Again, no Vitae cost.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Majesty.</b> It eliminates nearly all situational social penalties caused by your actions or appearance, while also granting a +1 (or more) bonus to Presence. Incredibly useful, and free.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Nightmare.</b> Not quite as useful as the others, but the illusions are fun. The Intimidation bonus is nice, and preventing Willpower from being spent to bolster actions is also useful during a Clash of Wills.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Obfuscate.</b> Incredibly useful. You're now almost impossible to track, and you can approach almost anywhere with people present without being noticed. You could even beat someone up in the middle of the street, and anyone other than your victim might not even realize it happened. Again, no Vitae cost.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Vigor.</b> +1 Strength and doubling your jump distance isn't particularly powerful on its own, but it's certainly useful during a chase.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Celerity.</b> +1 Defense isn't particularly exciting on its own, but the active effect is so absurdly powerful that almost everyone wants at least one dot of Celerity just to have a chance during a Clash of Wills if their action gets interrupted by Celerity. It's useful for much more than combat. I've used it alongside Dominate so the user could effectively &quot;win initiative,&quot; instantly lock eyes with a target who was intentionally avoiding eye contact, and gain just enough time to Mesmerize them. In a game where the initiator often wins, always acting first is extremely powerful.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Resilience.</b> Eh... this one is nice to have when you really need it, but it's also one I'm not particularly fond of. It's not bad, but it's not the Discipline I'd prioritize on a combat-focused character\u2014which also happens to be Gangrel's physical Discipline.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Here's why I proposed offering a Predatory Aspect at the first level: it's the fun part of Protean. It's also what the entire Discipline revolves around after the first dot: shapeshifting. (I'll come back to that later.)</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Every Discipline gets a cool power that's free or nearly free at its first dot. Why does Protean simply make you safer while you sleep?</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Maybe I'm just not imaginative enough. Maybe the real strength of Protean 1 is that it lets you extend your active hours by already being where you need to be the following night, or by allowing you to stay where you need to be until the very last minutes before sunrise. Eh... that's pretty anecdotal most of the time.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Beast's Skin</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I think I made my case poorly and focused too much on the Strength <i>Beast's Skin</i> can grant, rather than my main issue: how it discourages investing in physical traits for one of the most physically oriented clans.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Let's take three basic character concepts: the thug Gangrel, whom I'll call <b>Marie Blackwood</b>; the businessman Gangrel, whom I'll call <b>Steven Cash</b>; and the elder Gangrel, whom I'll call <b>John Gangrel</b> for his ridiculously high Attributes and Skills.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Let's assume Marie and Steven both have a bit of extra Experience to spend on clan Disciplines. Let's also ignore Merits, as they don't affect my argument.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Marie Blackwood, the Thug</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Attributes:</b> Int 1, Wits 3, Resolve 3, Str 4, Dex 2, Stam 3, Cha 2, Man 2, Com 2</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Skills</b></span><ul><li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Mental:</b> Academics 1, Occult 1, Politics 2</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Physical:</b> Athletics 3, Brawl 2, Larceny 2, Stealth 2, Survival 2</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Social:</b> Animal Ken 1, Intimidation 3, Socialize 1, Streetwise 2</span></li> </ul><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Disciplines:</b> Protean 3, Animalism 1, Resilience 1</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Steven Cash, the Gangrel Businessman</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Attributes:</b> Int 2, Wits 3, Resolve 2, Str 1, Dex 3, Stam 2, Cha 3, Man 3, Com 3</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Skills</b></span><ul><li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Mental:</b> Academics 2, Investigation 2, Politics 3</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Physical:</b> Drive 2, Firearms 2</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Social:</b> Animal Ken 1, Empathy 1, Intimidation 1, Persuasion 3, Socialize 2, Streetwise 1, Subterfuge 2</span></li> </ul><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Disciplines:</b> Protean 3, Animalism 2</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>John Gangrel, the Blood Potency 7 Elder</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Attributes:</b> Int 2, Wits 4, Resolve 3, Str 7, Dex 4, Stam 6, Cha 3, Man 1, Com 4</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Skills</b></span><ul><li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Mental:</b> Academics 2, Craft 3, Investigation 2, Occult 4, Politics 2</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Physical:</b> Athletics 4, Brawl 6, Stealth 4, Survival 5</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Social:</b> Animal Ken 4, Empathy 2, Intimidation 4, Socialize 2, Streetwise 1, Subterfuge 3</span></li> </ul><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Disciplines:</b> Protean 5, Resilience 5, Animalism 4, Vigor 3, Celerity 2, Nightmare 2, Auspex 1</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Now let's look at what happens when each of them transforms, you can probably guess where i'm going with this. I'll use three forms: a battle form, a stealth form, and a versatile form. </span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Bear \u2013 The Battle Form</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">For this form, I'm looking at increases to combat and survivability. I'll ignore Stealth. The bear has Str 6, Dex 2, Stam 4, Defense 5, 11 Health boxes, Brawl 4, and Athletics 3 (Climbing 3). At Size 7, it also has 11 Health boxes, but there's no real way of calculating how much Experience that Size increase is worth, so I'll ignore it. I'll also ignore Stealth and Survival since they aren't relevant here.</span><ul><li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Marie:</b> +2 Str, +1 Stam, +3 Health boxes, +2 Brawl, and a Climbing specialty. That's a total of roughly <b>13 XP</b> worth of combat ability. (Let's ignore Size since everyone gains the same benefit.)</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Steven:</b> +5 Str, -1 Dex, +2 Stam, +4 Health boxes, +2 Defense, +4 Brawl, +3 Athletics, and a Climbing specialty. That's roughly <b>39 XP</b> worth of combat ability\u2014three times as much as Marie, despite being the non-combat character.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>John Gangrel:</b> -1 Str, -2 Dex, -2 Stam, -3 Defense, -1 Athletics, -2 Brawl. That's roughly <b>-26 XP</b>. Shifting into the battle form somehow makes him weaker. In fact, every combat form granted by Protean 3 is weaker than his natural body.</span></li> </ul><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Domestic Cat \u2013 The Stealth Form</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">For this form, I'll consider increases to Athletics, Stealth, and Dexterity. Strength and Brawl don't really matter. The cat has Dex 5, Athletics 4, and Stealth 3.</span><ul><li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Marie:</b> +3 Dex, +1 Athletics, +1 Stealth, for roughly <b>16 XP</b> worth of stealth capabilities.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Steven: </b>+2 Dex, +4 Athletics, +3 Stealth, for roughly <b>22 XP</b> worth of stealth abilities\u2014again ahead of Marie.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>John Gangrel:</b> +1 Dex, -1 Stealth. A whopping <b>2 XP</b> gain, but his actual Stealth pool doesn't improve\u2014it remains 8. Looking like a cat certainly helps narratively, but mechanically his stealth doesn't improve, and his athletic capabilities are simply worse across the board.</span></li> </ul><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Large Dog \u2013 The Versatile Form</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">This form is useful all around. It's easy to obtain, reasonably capable in combat, and excellent at tracking. It also has the advantage of being a form you could realistically see almost anywhere, unlike a bear wandering through downtown.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">The dog has Str 4, Dex 4, Stam 3, Athletics 4 (Running), Brawl 3, Stealth 1, and Survival 3. Its Defense appears to be listed as 8, but it should probably be 7, and its bite pool is listed as 6 but should likely be 7. I'll assume both are meant to be 7.</span><ul><li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Marie:</b> +2 Dex, +1 Defense (capped at 6 by Wits 3), -1 Health box, +1 Athletics (Running), +1 Brawl, -1 Stealth, +1 Survival (Tracking). That's roughly <b>14 XP</b> worth of improvements. Her combat ability increases slightly at the cost of one Health box, her Stealth pool increases by 1 if based on Dexterity, and she becomes a bit better at tracking.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Steven:</b> +3 Str, +1 Dex, +1 Stam, +3 Defense (capped by Wits 3), +4 Athletics (Running), +3 Brawl, +1 Stealth, +3 Survival (Tracking). That's roughly <b>44 XP</b> worth of abilities\u2014a straight upgrade across the board.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>John Gangrel:</b> -2 Str, -3 Stam, -4 Health boxes, -3 Brawl, -3 Stealth, -2 Survival. As expected, another straight downgrade, losing roughly <b>36 XP</b> worth of capability.</span></li> </ul><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Let's move away from comparisons and instead use an example from my own group's experience.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I'll use the actual Gangrel character in my game. I'm perfectly happy with the character, and the player seems to be having fun too, so I'm not complaining. His character has no combat Skills, and his physical Attributes are fairly mediocre before Disciplines. He has access to the bear form.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">The Gangrel, along with the rest of his group, got into a fight with three revenants, partly because the idealistic Gangrel tried to stop them from feeding on a dying mortal and announced his presence beforehand. The fight began, he was grappled, and then shifted into bear form. (I allow the use of Disciplines while grappled. I'll get back to that later.)</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">The revenants had Brawl 3, Strength 3, and Vigor 2. They weren't pushovers.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">It didn't matter in the end. The bear's 11-die grapple pool (including Vigor 1) was more than enough to overpower them.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">The scene was messy. The Gangrel and his group broke the Masquerade, received a strike from the Sheriff, and barely managed to contain the breach before it got out of hand.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">All in all, it was a fun scene. The players enjoyed it, the Gangrel became addicted to Vitae, and he got to feel like a badass. I'm glad everyone had fun.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Now let's imagine a different scenario.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Suppose the player had instead been a Daeva with Strength 4, Brawl 3, and Vigor 3\u2014a character heavily invested in being a brawler.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Now imagine they get into a feud with Steven, the businessman Gangrel. Steven has been getting on their nerves, actively undermining their investments and picking fights on his own turf: the social arena.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Eventually the player says, &quot;Screw it. I'll challenge him to a duel at Elysium so we can settle this on my battlefield.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Elysium duel night arrives.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Steven shifts into a bear and suddenly has around a 50% chance of completely wrecking the dedicated combat character simply by becoming a top-tier fighter with a single instant action.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">That would feel pretty bad.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Anyway, I don't want to spend all day discussing white-room scenarios, so I'll move on to my conclusion regarding <i>Beast's Skin</i>.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Conclusion</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">The mechanics of Protean 3 actively discourage investing in an entire branch of Attributes and Skills.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">For characters who avoid physical investment, the deal is simply too good. For those who already invested heavily, the power ranges from underwhelming to outright detrimental.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">If we compare Marie and Steven, Marie might reasonably ask:</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">&quot;Why does Steven get everything I have, even though he invested nothing into physical traits?&quot;</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Sure, Marie can fight outside her animal form, which might prevent a Masquerade breach if combat happens unexpectedly.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">However, once both she and Steven transform, all of her investment becomes largely irrelevant. Meanwhile, Steven can invest in Politics, Persuasion, command animals better than she can, and still gain nearly all the benefits of Protean 3.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">The utility of the actual animal forms, however, remains unchanged.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I'll now answer the other points regarding <i>Beast's Skin</i>.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Finding Animals</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">This is entirely dependent on the territory, but bears are fairly easy to find throughout most northern and mountainous regions. Large cats are also fairly common in some areas.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Finding the animal itself should be relatively easy by using a dog form with Survival 3 and a Tracking specialty. Finding a dog, meanwhile, is incredibly easy.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><i>Feral Whispers</i> also solves the problem of convincing an animal to approach you so it can be drained, while the Kiss ensures it won't fight back.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Humanity</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Humanity doesn't really factor into my criticism.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">My issue isn't that the power is too strong or too weak. My issue is that it completely ignores a character's experience investment.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Vitae &amp; Blood Potency</b></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Sure, spending 2 Vitae isn't free. I'll grant that. It's also not particularly expensive.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">The time required to transform only really matters if you don't have the opportunity to prepare beforehand, since you can remain in animal form for as long as you like.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Animal Form and Equipment</b></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">It's true that you lose access to your equipment while transformed, but that's the price you pay for actually becoming an animal.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">In return, you gain passive discretion, easier access to restricted areas, and entirely different methods of movement.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Primeval Miasma</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Rethinking about it, I'm honestly mostly fine with this power. As a natural peak of shapeshift being able to become formless is fine. Idk, maybe Force of Nature would also make a pretty cool capstone, even though it could become redundant.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Solutions</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I don't know if I actually want to stick with &quot;animal form.&quot; Gangrel certainly have an animalistic and feral flair, but Protean 2, 4, and 5 are actually closer to a weird mix of Vicissitude. Maybe animal forms could instead become a Protean/Animalism Devotion. Honestly, I'm not entirely sure what I want, other than wanting Protean to follow a stronger thematic progression while also feeling mechanically satisfying.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Protean 1</span></b><br /> My idea for Protean 1 was to give players a taste of the Discipline as early as possible. Every other Discipline does that. Protean doesn't.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Moving Unmarked Grave as a Resilience 1 + Protean 1 devotion would allow any Gangrel to become a nomad with very little investment.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I could also move unmarked grave into my rework of protean 1 and add it as adaptation. Maybe Adaptations could be more than animalistic trait, but focus on adaptation, including, but not limited to unmarked grave.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Protean 3</span></b><br /> The idea here is that you would use it to reshape your abilities rather than replace them. You wouldn't lose the value of what you've already invested in. Instead, you'd gain increased versatility or become better at what you've specialized in, without overshadowing characters who have invested Experience into that same area.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">The only issue I can think of is that a rat with Strength 6 would definitely be on the weird side. Maybe I could cap Strength based on Size or make Size influence Strength in some way. I don't know\u2014I could probably devise something there. My previous example was only a draft.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Honestly, some kind of vampiric biokinesis, \u00e0 la Vicissitude, could also be cool. However, moving in that direction would take Gangrel somewhere I'm not sure I'd like them to go. I like that they're wild and animalistic. Besides, transforming into animals is a classic vampire power.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Grapple Tangent</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">This is probably influenced by how we play at my table, but we've found that grappling is by far the strongest combat style for one simple reason: it effectively locks the target out of their turn.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">It's also fairly easy to initiate a grapple. Attack pools scale much faster than Defense. At worst, you spend a point of Willpower, and you're probably looking at somewhere between a 60% and 95% chance of successfully initiating the grapple.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Brawlers can invest almost everything into Strength and Brawl, and that's really all they need. A Weaponry specialist, on the other hand, also has to invest in Brawl, otherwise they risk never getting the opportunity to actually use their Weaponry Skill. On top of that, weapons suffer Initiative penalties, so the stronger your weapon, the greater the chance you'll be grappled before you can act, effectively losing your turn.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Vampires can also bite as part of a grapple while frenzying, which deals lethal damage to other vampires, making biting one of the strongest low-investment anti-vampire options available. It's not foolproof\u2014you'll probably end up addicted to Vitae\u2014but it's still incredibly effective, especially against other vampires.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">When is grappling <i>not</i> effective? When you're fighting multiple enemies.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Of course, fighting multiple enemies is always dangerous regardless of your build, so at that point you should probably be looking for a way to disengage anyway.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">This is why I allow grappled characters to take mental actions, such as activating Disciplines, at a -3 penalty. It at least gives them something to do without completely invalidating grappling as a strategy.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Maybe I should even make this a general rule. After all, if I still have a knife in my hand and the grappler doesn't spend their action controlling my weapon or pinning my arm, why shouldn't I at least be able to try stabbing them in the side?</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\">Actual Conclusion</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Anyway, this ended up being far longer than I intended to write, and it's probably still pretty rambly.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Hopefully I make sense, thank you for the input.</span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-14T15:45:09-07:00","id":1561695,"title":"","content":"Without trying to pick out individual things:<br /> <br /> 1) I do think there is a level where your house rules and specific play style makes Protean as-is less &quot;right&quot; than it is in other approaches. Something that feels completely skipped when it comes to Unmarked Grave is that it's an entirely different feeding strategy. Low BP Gangrel and just park themself under where a local predator eats, or under something like a local farm that slaughters animals on-site, and passively sate their need for blood. Factor in Animalism or a ghoul and a Gangrel basically never has to worry about keeping their daily hunger in check until their BP starts to go up. Protean 5 also increasing feed possibilities... and these bonuses aren't going to show up if you go for a more abstract feeding mechanic and don't give them something to make up for it. Making Unmarked Grave less useful for feeding also reduces its thematic connection to the Beast.<br /> <br /> 2) There is also going to be a huge subjective difference for those of us that have been playing the Vampire games for awhile. If you think VtR 2e Protean has issues... VtM and VtR 1e were worse on pretty much every level. So for a lot of people it's a massive improvement on mechanics, themes, progression, and so on. There's a 35 year history of have Protean &quot;is&quot; in Vampire, and the VtR 2e authors had to balance that with a desire to make it fit with the changes they wanted to make. Could the VtR 2e gone a bit farther? Probably. But then there's always a balancing act for an existing property. What someone new to the games finds annoying can be a core concept to older players that they'd hate to see changed too much.<br /> <br /> 3) I think your analysis of Beast's Skin undermines your point more than it makes it. Why invest in physical Skills? Because your natural potential is higher than those of mundane animals. Sure, it's a great savings for a non-combat character to be able to whip out a bunch of decent combat stats, or a fighter-type to have a sneaky form, but over time the utility of non-human forms more narratively is going to be the main benefit as you can easily overcome the stat disparities over time. Combat capable Elders are going to be able to easily take down an overly confident young Gangrel that thinks turning into a bear is all the power they'll ever need.<br /> <br /> On the other side of this, if you make the animal forms too additive... the numbers get ridiculous pretty fast. As you noted a Str 6 rat isn't really where you'd want this to go.<br /> <br /> That said, I do get the overall point. I think the easiest home brew for your specific needs would be to give 9-again if your Attributes or Skill is lower in the animal form, or 8-again if both. These aren't the hardest bonuses to get, but it addresses the core issue of wanting synergy over feeling like you can just skip buying certain things. A high Strength vampire in a bear form can be more dangerous than a low Strength one that's trying to have a combat back-up power.<br /> <br /> 4) Grappling is powerful... but again play style matters a lot here. Plus Celerity laughs in the face of grapple focused fighters. When the situations are right, grappling is brutal (but that's also true in reality), when they're wrong it's practically suicidal. Numbers are certainly a simple way to make grappling horrible, but grapplers also need speed to reach their opponents, high initiatives to make sure they go first (the idea that you can just invest in Str and Brawl falls apart there), and have given up on most forms of defense against ranged attacks besides ducking behind things which interferes with closing on targets.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-14T18:45:54-07:00","id":1561698,"title":"","content":"Not going to do quoteboxes both because formatting and I suspect it'd produce a double notification, but synthesizing or hitting the highlights:<br /> <br /> The thread I made was created primarily because Requiem has quietly emphasized throughout its run that the Kindred are a convergent phenomenon arrived at through a number of different routes (&quot;vampirism is a wound&quot; is a particularly apt way Rose Bailey put it years ago, analogizing it to how there's lots of different ways to cut your finger that nevertheless look mostly the same), but at the time it seemed odd to me that such a variable series of channels of monstrosity could produce such consistent chains of power development despite development or integration of other powersets (e.g. blood sorcery and other covenant tricks, bloodline gifts, etc) being an established phenomenon.<br /> <br /> Most of half a decade of time to stew on other gamelines, including Deviant: the Renegades (whose protagonists are similarly only consistently unified by the root elements of &quot;your superpowers are part and parcel of your having a broken soul or something that acts like one that messes with your ability to relate to people&quot; but who use the same basic mechanics for their Variations regardless of where they come from thanks to a very modular power system), means I have an easier time squaring that circle now than back then \u2014 the powers don't have to actually be the exact same thing in setting metaphysics to work the same and the powers associated with vampirism have a very &quot;close enough that they bleed into each other&quot; tendency anyway, to the point of suggesting various modern covenant powers have direct equivalents in other places and times.<br /> <br /> Vampire's developed powers run on the logic of accumulation and lines of descent and tutelage as part of the undeath-and-blood thematics, which means mechanizing an equivalent of Awakened mages' ability to improvise effects within the scope of the Arcana they know, even at a slower rate of development, would suggest things about the intended mode of play that aren't true and ultimately add unnecessary steps and wordcount to &quot;in the rare event that a table needs supernatural tools that aren't covered by the existing Disciplines, blood sorcery, and any Devotions that are published or devised by the group, they can create a Discipline for use in their chronicle&quot; \u2014 the corebook already had to cut a bunch of material on bloodlines for space and the big picture tendency in Chronicles is for magic and/or groups of people to facilitate tweaking the rules.<br /> <br /> With apologies for leaning heavily on comparisons to other gamelines, Deviant and Mage share a notable distinction from Vampire in that their developed powers are the majority of their supernatural kit; this isn't the case at all for Requiem's vampires, which is both 1. why Majesty is less &quot;a power of the Daeva&quot; and more &quot;a quality of the Daeva&quot; (and their trio of clan Disciplines including two always-on physicals) that means most of the stuff they do as vampires is just being really good at stuff every vampire can technically do (be a compelling figure who deals magical drugs and binds people to their will through manipulation and charisma and spend most of their Vitae on upkeep), and 2. why the Gangrel still have Resilience as their physical Discipline (they're the clan most associated with frenzy and frenzy gives a number of consistent buffs for violence in 2e).<br /> <br /> The Kindred aren't relying on learning Disciplines for the bulk of their effectiveness as actors in the world, even if any given dot is broadly useful in one way or another, and diving too deep into supernatural qualities for your effectiveness is alternately a very unstable unlife or a losing bet altogether. While the line isn't running on a character's clan being an archetypal straitjacket by any stretch of the imagination, it's still pretty clear that the Nosferatu and the Gangrel tend to be on the lower end of vampiric society for the simple reason that they're less suited to playing along with the political pretenses that their less socially disadvantaged fellows engage with to do institutional corruption.<br /> <br /> The need to belong to a bloodline to get one of the five core proprietary clan Disciplines without risking giving another vampire asymmetrical leverage over you means most Kindred are going to think twice about pursuing dots in Auspex, Dominate, Majesty, Nightmare, or Protean without an inroad to learning them already, especially when they could instead commit their resources in a more effective manner. Nightmare and Protean's bigger tricks are the less situationally useful ones because the Nosferatu and the Gangrel have situational dominance that is harder to deploy without risk, being the creepy unreality-adjacent vampires and the volatile shapeshifting vampires instead of the glamorous pop-culture vampires, the distant and commanding vampires, or the shadowy vampires who know all your secrets. Protean is the least subtle of the proprietary Disciplines and it's attached to the clan with the easiest time being in places where there aren't a lot of people around.<br /> <br /> The argument that Humanity doesn't matter and that you can spend all your time in animal form for better stats is fundamentally ignoring that the game is written such that playing a character who spends all their time as a dog is going to run into problems that will eventually require them to assume a form that can talk or turn a doorknob unless they want Animal Control after them and that having your Humanity atrophy from lack of meaningful human contact is going to mean spending more time as a very violent animal, especially if you aren't staying fed well enough to risk penalizing your frenzy rolls further (and potentially triggering them more often).<br /> <br /> The fact that your ability to throw a punch doesn't translate to your ability to claw someone as a bear or bite them as a dog means you either invest Experiences in learning how to fight normally or you avoid fights where you can't be in an animal form. History and evolution have narrowed the viability of characters who can only be competent in a fight in narrow contexts and Vampire is not primarily a game about fights, and it's honestly kind of weird to treat the stats of an alternate form as &quot;worth Experiences&quot; in a game where Vigor and Resilience fully duplicate the benefits of buying an Attribute dot and then some even without an in-clan cost-break, and those don't go away when you have to talk to someone. This is already not a game that incentivizes buying Strength and Stamina through normal channels.<br /> <br /> The Gangrel Businessman brings in a different problem, which is that it's not the game's fault for not locking in clan archetypes so hard as to prevent players from building a Savage whose sire Embraced them into the clan despite having below-average Strength and no non-technological Physical Skills. More power to Steven for not being stigmatized for turning into a bear in the middle of Elysium, but I don't find it terribly out of sorts for the shapeshifting power that lets you assume the shape of a predator to come with the instincts to be able to effectively use their body to do predation.<br /> <br /> Blood and Smoke being one of the earliest Revised Storytelling System products means there's lots of little things that could stand to be mechanized more effectively \u2014 Animalism 3 effectively serving as a massive range-extender before Lord of the Land caps things off comes to mind \u2014 but I consider Protean's ladder of effects pretty solid in concept as-is.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":111,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","user":"Satchel","date":".  Fixed more typos. 07-15-2026, 07:44 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-16T14:29:13-07:00","id":1561782,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Again, lots of good point being made.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Response to Heavy Arms</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">1) I must admit I hadn\u2019t thought about Unmarked Grave as a feeding strategy on its own; it\u2019s a pretty creative use. I still wouldn\u2019t be excited by the power on its own, at best its an easy justification for the herd merit. I am aware that protean is a full kit. I still think Unmarked grave would be better as a devotion to those that truly want it. </span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">2) Of course. I also played vampire for about 20 years. It started with a VTM LARP at my local Youth Club back in my early adolescence, where I played as Toreador ex-crusader-Knight or something like this. Man, that was a different time, lol. I don\u2019t think there\u2019s a universal right or wrong answer to what I perceive. I\u2019m also fully aware that\u2019s it\u2019s an issue I have, which some might or might not share, not a failure on the part of the developers.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">3) For my analysis, I\u2019ll guess we\u2019ll have to agree to disagree. The amount of investment it takes to surpass the discipline is only really assignable at Elder power level. Before that, as soon as one transforms, they lose all their ability.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">4) I don\u2019t dislike your proposed solution; it has some charm with the added benefit of being an incredibly simple fix. I\u2019ll think about it.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">If I do end up deciding to go in a \u2018\u2019build your own form\u2019\u2019 direction, I\u2019d probably go in the direction that your stats must closely resemble the form you\u2019re trying to assume, so you wouldn\u2019t have a bear with STR 2 or a rat with strength 6. I'd restrict the strength maximum to size, or size +1. If the forms are built prior to being used, extra complexity won\u2019t factor in during play too much.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">In CoD, strength gets weird when you factor in non-human characters. 6 dots of strength for a bear, or 9 for an elephant, doesn\u2019t really account for how much stronger they are compared to humans, or how weaker a size 1 animal would be. There is no world in which a human gets wrestled by a cat, but, unless I missed something, the house cat is better at grappling than a regular human. Not saying it\u2019s a fault with the system, it wasn\u2019t designed to handle those kinds of silly corner cases.</span><ol class=\"decimal\"><li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I think it is really powerful in my setting for a few reasons :</span><ol class=\"decimal\"><li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Guns are rare in Japan. I decided to honour that. I think, in around 3 years of active campaign, guns were involved, maybe once in a big Yakuza raid some players\u2019 characters took part in. </span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Fights are rare and are often against a similar number of opponents. My players\u2019 characters have some very good stealth capabilities, supernaturally even, so they pretty much never pick a fight they aren\u2019t certain to win, and I respect that.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Nearly all fights that broke off were shady street spontaneous brawls; range weapons don\u2019t shine in that environment. </span></li> </ol></li> </ol><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">As for investment what I meant was that all fighters need to invest in defence to some extent, but a weaponry based fighter needs to invest in weaponry, but also brawl to avoid being locked out of their fighting method.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">One of my fellow ST and I are heavily considering granting a free interrupt chance to anyone carrying a weapon against a grappler they could intercept. Basically, it would allow you to take your next instant action before the grappler attempts to grab you to allow you to make your attack. If you have a knife in your hand and someone tries to close in, it shouldn\u2019t be <i>that </i>hard to stab them in return.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Response to Satchel</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>On the other thread.</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Ah, are you referring to multiple sources of vampirism stated in the game's lore? Like how multiple bloodlines seem to have spontaneously appeared from difference sources?</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I will admit I tend not to look too much into that stuff, not that it isn\u2019t interesting, but it isn\u2019t generally pertinent to how I run my games. I\u2019m more interested in how theories of kindred origins say more about those who hold them as truth says about themselves rather than vampirism on its own.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>On the Powers of Vampires</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">In general I agree with that statement : Vampire power does not correlate directly with their discipline. Some are more useful than others : Auspex, Dominate &amp; Majesty are far more useful than Nightmare &amp; Protean, in the various areas in which vampires tend to shine.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>On Humanity &amp; Shapeshifting Duration</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I\u2019m really not saying that one should spend all their time shapeshifted into animals. I\u2019m really not. What I meant was that, sure, it cost 2 Vitae to transform; it\u2019s not cheap, which is why it's even more encouraging to make sure you make the most out of it.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">As for Humanity, I don\u2019t really consider it as part of the equation because my issue with the discipline simply stems from the fact that it ignores character abilities in a way that the vast majority of disciplines don\u2019t. (Possession is also like this, which i also don\u2019t like, but possession is another thing completly, and a discipline at 5 dots)</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">All different character with access to the discipline also have to deal with humanity. If we take the sample characters; Marie also has less tools to resolve conflict, she might also have to take actions that will take a toll on her humanity, while Steven can afford to negotiate with a better chance of success.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Clan Stereotype vs Outlier</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Let\u2019s go back to Marie Blackwood, the stereotypical savage, and Steven, the clan outlier.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Is one character strictly better than the other, no of course not. Both have their respective strengths and weaknesses and both are valid. However, Steven certainly has access to more tools for the same investment, while Marie simply doubles down into making sure she has a plan B should her form not be possible to adopt.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Steven can persuade, socialize, and is a pretty good investigator, while Marie\u2019s skills involve typical gangrel stuff. Steven also has firearms, so it\u2019s not like he can\u2019t fight at all either. I\u2019d say he\u2019s even a more versatile fighter; he simply has to be a bit more aware of his surroundings than Marie, but he's also better at it.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">When they both transform, they invest resources to deal with a problem; all what Marie learned, all her previous experience, stops being relevant. Steven is as good as a fighter as her and I, personally, don\u2019t like that.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">In an ideal world, both character skills would still be a factor they shapeshift so they can show their individual strength instead of becoming a copy pasta of a particular animal. It actually does so for disciplines, so why are attribute skills also ignored?</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Another idea I very much subscribe to his ; Flavour is free.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Meaning, I can make both Steven and Marie into compelling characters with interesting stories and personalities. If I want to have fun with Protean, however, one is more incentivized than the other, and it\u2019s the clan outlier, which I find weird. I\u2019m not saying that\u2019s <i>all </i>that factors in, but there\u2019s certainly an incentive to do so.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I\u2019m all for outliers existing, they often make good character concept. I just don\u2019t think they should be mechanically favoured over the others. Low intelligence Ventrue or Mekhet will struggle, a socially inept Daeva can help themselves a bit with Majesty, but it can\u2019t do everything on it\u2019s own.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Besides, since combat <i>is </i>rare, there\u2019s even more incentive to branch out of physical skills and invest in social and mental skills.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">And before this is used as a counter-argument, I\u2019m perfectly aware that the game doesn\u2019t really care about balance; mental disciplines are very strong, a lot of merit combos are also very strong. I can deal with that. I can ask players to cut back on combos, but if I have to fight the urge to make a clan outlier because it feels more rewarding, I just don\u2019t like it.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">To me that would be like if Majesty 3 base power let you copy the social attributes of a mortal you drank. We\u2019d get fighters Daeva that would ignore their social skills and chase their local politicians, like Pok\u00e9mons.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">My idea of a well-written shapeshifting power would be to have it still allow Steven to shapeshift to become better at what he struggles in his base form while still respecting Marie for investing in her physical skills, respecting and respecting her clan stereotypes.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">This is basically how Pathfinder 1e redesigned shapeshifting from D&amp;D 3.5. Shape shifting gave some abilities: a boost to some stats and a reduction to others based on size changes. Meaning, druids who wanted to be good at fighting, while transformed, had to devote energy to it. Stealthy druids needed to invest in dexterity and stealth to make the most out of their smaller shapes, and less physical druids would still get something out shapeshifting, but they wouldn't be able to be better casters than the physical druids while also matching their melee capabilities.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b><span style=\"font-size:14px\">On Blood and Smokes Mechanical efficiency</span></b></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Oh, indeed, there will always be things that will need fixing in my eyes. I\u2019m a perpetual homebrewer. Hell, i even revisit what i make all the time. I like tuning mechanics and rethinking how something can be made to optimize the mechanical output I\u2019m looking for.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Especially as it pertains to the effect of written mechanics on play experience.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">It\u2019s certainly why I appreciated V5 Hunger dice so much as first. I found the mechanical representation of hunger dice a far better way to represent and make you feel the struggle with an uncontrollable beast that tries to wrestle the reins from you than the \u2018\u2019Blood Magic Points\u2019\u2019 all previous vampire gamelines had.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b><span style=\"font-size:18px\">To all</span></b></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Thanks for the response,</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I\u2019ll probably cook something on my own before I start another chronicle.</span><br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-16T16:18:59-07:00","id":1561788,"title":"","content":"What I find odd is the certainty that your PCs will have access to shapeshift almost every fight.<br /> <br /> D&amp;D and Pathfinder need shapeshifting rules balanced as they are because they assume the character suffers no special penalty for using it whenever they want. Most of the action takes place away from cities anyway, if this is even a concern.<br /> <br /> But Protean was made for a game where this is a particularly hard power to use because of context. Your description is of a group where the Gangrel takes bear form anywhere, for any reason, without repercussions. What the fuck!?<br /> <br /> Japan isn't a place where bears walk the street freely in big cities. T\u014dky\u014d definitely isn't like that. Even if no one sees the transformation, <b>it is a bear</b>. Authorities will come because of this alone, and they'll arrive fast anywhere in T\u014dky\u014d.<br /> <br /> That is the main reason why we don't see that big of a deal, or at least I don't. IME few fights are conducive of using Protean to turn into a bear, it's not exactly a subtle animal, and if the Gangrel in question avoids fights unless that's possible, they're already paying by not having a way to pick many fights, while a combat build would allow for more fights.<br /> <br /> Protean simply isn't a &quot;combat power&quot;, this is a rather unusual strategy to rely on with such certainty.<br /> <br /> Another thing I'm noticing by how much you repeat that your players only fight when they want is that you kind of locked yourself in a tight spot: you want balanced combat, but you deliberately run the game in a way your players only fight in optimal conditions.<br /> <br /> Literally every reason why Protean or Grapple aren't problems fall apart because whenever they don't have optimal conditions, your players must don't fight. You won't get what you want that way, because whatever you do, you'll just change the fights they pick, and those fights will be boring and extremely one-sided.<br /> <br /> It's not the rules that are getting in your way. Is the expectation of an interesting combat when your game is completely tuned to never let an interesting combat even start.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-16T18:04:54-07:00","id":1561798,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Not going to lie, this kind of answer really make me doubt my ability to communicate properly.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Since the same point have been repeated multiple time, I\u2019ll take the blame. I spent too many words on rationalizing a feeling when I should have stated it in simpler word.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Don\u2019t take my quoting as an attempt to pick out your post, I\u2019m doing it for the sake of clarity.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">But before I do, I\u2019ll state my issue with Beast\u2019s skin in the clearest manner I can.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><i>Beast\u2019s skin ignore character physical stats and skill investment, I don\u2019t like this, it goes against some of my core personal design principle. </i></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">That it\u2019s.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1561664-protean-a-rant-and-some-homebrewed-solutions-looking-for-opinions?p=1561788#post1561788\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What I find odd is the certainty that your PCs will have access to shapeshift almost every fight.</div> </div> </div> </div></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I don\u2019t assume that.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1561664-protean-a-rant-and-some-homebrewed-solutions-looking-for-opinions?p=1561788#post1561788\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> D&amp;D and Pathfinder need shapeshifting rules balanced as they are because they assume the character suffers no special penalty for using it whenever they want. Most of the action takes place away from cities anyway, if this is even a concern.</div> </div> </div> </div></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I stated pathfinder as an example of a shapeshifting I liked according to my taste.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1561664-protean-a-rant-and-some-homebrewed-solutions-looking-for-opinions?p=1561788#post1561788\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> But Protean was made for a game where this is a particularly hard power to use because of context. Your description is of a group where the gangrel takes bear form anywhere, for any reason, without repercussions. What the fuck!?</div> </div> </div> </div></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I\u2019m also aware.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">So is my Gangrel player who got hit with a massive masquerade breach for shifting as a bear in a street brawl. Fortunately for him, his coterie had access to a lot of merit to cover up the whole story, but they did not step out of that scene socially unscathed.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I\u2019m not saying the Bear form is easily available. Hence why I also gave the Large Dog as an example, which is also a more believable street fighting form.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">And before anyone reply that I do think it\u2019s available in all moments : It\u2019s not.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1561664-protean-a-rant-and-some-homebrewed-solutions-looking-for-opinions?p=1561788#post1561788\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Japan isn't a place where bears walk the street freely in big cities. T\u014dky\u014d definitely isn't like that. Even if no one sees the transformation, <b>it is a bear</b>. Authorities will come because of this alone, and they'll arrive fast anywhere in T\u014dky\u014d.</div> </div> </div> </div></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I want to be cheeky and respond that: <i>Hmm actually</i> a bear has attacked a supermarket in Numata City in Gunma Prefecture on the 7th of October 2025, probably some crazed gangrel.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">But more seriously, I\u2019m aware.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">This is exactly what happened in the previous scene I detailed bellow.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">After 4 rounds of combat with revenants (about 15 seconds) a lady noticed and called the police. They only got saved thanks to the other player contact and influence.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1561664-protean-a-rant-and-some-homebrewed-solutions-looking-for-opinions?p=1561788#post1561788\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> That is the main reason why we don't see that big of a deal, or at least I don't. IME few fights are conducive of using Protean to turn into a bear, it's not exactly a subtle animal, and if the gangrel in question avoids fights unless that's possible, they're already paying by not having a way to pick many fights, while a combat build would allow for more fights.</div> </div> </div> </div></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I agree.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I\u2019m also not saying beast's skin is only useful for battle. It has myriad of uses.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1561664-protean-a-rant-and-some-homebrewed-solutions-looking-for-opinions?p=1561788#post1561788\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Protean simply isn't a &quot;combat power&quot;, this is a rather unusual strategy to rely on with such certainty.</div> </div> </div> </div></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Also agree, combat is only one of its components, but it seems like every criticism has focused on combat, so I\u2019ll reiterate my issue with Beast\u2019s Skin.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><i>Beast\u2019s skin ignore character physical stats and skill investment, I don\u2019t like this. Whether It goes to their advantage or disadvantage. </i></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1561664-protean-a-rant-and-some-homebrewed-solutions-looking-for-opinions?p=1561788#post1561788\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Another thing I'm noticing by how much you repeat that your players only fight when they want is that you kind of locked yourself in a tight spot: you want balanced combat\u2026</div> </div> </div> </div></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I don\u2019t want balanced combat, if it happens it happens, if it doesn\u2019t, it doesn\u2019t. Chronicles fights aren\u2019t balanced. A few of the mental disciplines can end a fight with one or two simple rolls. I\u2019m really fine with it.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1561664-protean-a-rant-and-some-homebrewed-solutions-looking-for-opinions?p=1561788#post1561788\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> but you deliberately run the game in a way your players only fight in optimal conditions.</div> </div> </div> </div></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">First, I don\u2019t deliberately run the game in that way. This is a consequence of respecting my players\u2019 actions. They take care not getting caught, they invest in stealth options, in the safe place merit. Most of them are careful before they act, and I honor that.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I run a sandbox campaign, combat happens if it happens, I don\u2019t plan \u2018\u2019combat scenes\u2019\u2019. I plan plot kits and combat is but one way to solve a plot kit, often the messiest.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">The gangrel got cornered last time because he very clearly announced himself to a pack of angry revenants and tried to keep his moral high ground by giving them a chance; It turned very poorly, very quickly and he had two choices : Dying or Risking a masquerade breach.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1561664-protean-a-rant-and-some-homebrewed-solutions-looking-for-opinions?p=1561788#post1561788\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Literally every reason why Protean or Grapple aren't problems fall apart because whenever they don't have optimal conditions, your players must don't fight. You won't get what you want that way, because whatever you do, you'll just change the fights they pick, and those fights will be boring and extremely one-sided.</div> </div> </div> </div></span><br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">In my experience, in VTR, grapple is king of melee and weaponry struggles. Locking out someone of their turn is simply the best strategy. You get to deal damage and they don\u2019t get to play; that\u2019s the winning defensive move that allows you to keep dealing damage.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">If you fight multiple enemies, it\u2019s dangerous, but fighting multiple enemies is never going to be advantageous. I\u2019m not sure weaponry would help you much either way, because honestly, what risks happening, is that the side with the most combatant will have one attempt to grapple you while the other one with a weapons wack you while you are defenseless.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Sure, someone can both invest in brawl and weaponry, but even then, to add insult to injury; Some Brawl weapon exists. They ain't the best, but they can still render your punches lethal if you need it.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1561664-protean-a-rant-and-some-homebrewed-solutions-looking-for-opinions?p=1561788#post1561788\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> It's not the rules that are getting in your way. Is the expectation of an interesting combat when your game is completely tuned to never let an interesting combat even start.</div> </div> </div> </div></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Again, just to be clear, I don\u2019t have the expectation nor the desire to create \u2018\u2019an interesting combat\u2019\u2019 if it happens naturally in the game, cool, I\u2019ll remember it fondly. But I won\u2019t force it.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Heck, I think that even if it devolved into an absolute fuck fest, the scene where my Gangrel player frenzied, turned into a bear to fight off the revenants while the other players where scrambling to help him and minimize the masquerade breach, was certainly a cool scene.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">The revenants were certainly dangerous, but I don\u2019t kid myself; they never stood a chance. And honestly, good, I don\u2019t want to end 125 hours of campaign on a balanced combat. I don't want to kill a PC simply because an encounter had to be balanced.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">It had nothing to do with the rules of combat and everything to do with everything around it.</span><ul><li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">The fact that each round the neighborhood was getting increasingly aware that a fucking bear was fighting with Yakuza in a side street of Shibuya. </span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">The Mekhet character got infected with Malkavia as part of plotkit that can evolve into a bloodline later. They are trying to hide it and are trying to avoid spreading the disease. I made it spread through vitae and mental disciplines. Which was very stress inducing when they nearly got bit by one of the frenzied revenants, barely saving themselves by interrupting celerity to get out of the way.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">The Nosferatu (Von Schreck bloodline) at the end decided to use Majesty on the onlooker to try to attract attention on themselves and make them believe it was all part of very realistic movie set. I ended up telling them: </span><ul><li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><i>\u2018\u2019 This is the dumbest plan I ever heard as you start to get the attention on yourself and the scene, people get out their phone to start to film. If you roll an exceptional success, they will be dumbstruck enough to forget to film, if you don\u2019t get an exceptional success, you\u2019ll be everywhere online next to a bear with 3 rapidly rotting cadavers. It\u2019s going to be exceptional one way or the other, good or bad\u2018\u2019</i></span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Lo and behold, after spending willpower they managed to roll 6 success and get the exceptional success. </span></li> </ul></li> </ul><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Again, cool scene, it had a battle which enhanced the chronicles and included the whole range of protean 3 advantages and drawback when used for combat.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">The players managed to avoid the worse through careful use of their influence, allies and everything, they didn\u2019t come out unscathed; They all got hit with an official strike from the Sheriff and they owe her a massive boon for getting her involved, which she definitely plan on using in the future.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I guess that my post if very ranting but I\u2019ll also add this :</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I\u2019m having fun, no where in my current campaign I\u2019m struggling. I don\u2019t necessarily like that grapple is often the most effective battle tactic, but that\u2019s like\u2026 such a little part of the current campaign.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I\u2019m going to repeat myself again, not to be pedantic, but because I\u2019ve been to communicate my feeling.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><i>Beast\u2019s skin ignores character physical stats and skill investment; I don\u2019t like this. Whether it goes to their advantage or disadvantage.</i></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I originally asked opinions simply to know if others had had the same issues and if anyone had came with solutions. I instead spent more energy trying to justify my feelings and opinions than actually coming with solutions.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Again, I take the blame, my post definitely registered as ''debate mode'' even though I wasn\u2019t trying to convince anyone to change their mind; I was trying to simply explain my point of view.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Anyway, thanks for taking the time to answer, appreciated.</span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26771,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","user":"Algarik","date":". 07-16-2026, 09:07 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-16T18:51:22-07:00","id":1561801,"title":"","content":"Apologies for the glut of boxes to follow that have been partially covered by other posts while I've been writing this one:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Algarik</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1561664-protean-a-rant-and-some-homebrewed-solutions-looking-for-opinions?p=1561782#post1561782\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ah, are you referring to multiple sources of vampirism stated in the game's lore? Like how multiple bloodlines seem to have spontaneously appeared from difference sources?</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm referring to how the Jiang Shi's elders are different from other Kindred in a way that their descendants aren't, or how there's a loose metaphysical reason that all Kindred vampires use the same base mechanics and have the ability to learn each other's Disciplines, that being the same bit of magical logic that makes a vampire and their Feeding Grounds reflective of each other and makes a vampire's Humanity rating approximately discernible from their appearance.<br /> <br /> I had wondered why proximity-based gradual resemblance didn't produce a more fluid default power system for the Kindred before recognizing that the inertia that produces those consistent powers is stronger than can be overcome on a regular enough scale to distinguish vampiric innovation from the existing means of homebrew for the line.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> On Humanity &amp; Shapeshifting Duration<br /> I\u2019m really not saying that one should spend all their time shapeshifted into animals. I\u2019m really not. What I meant was that, sure, it cost 2 Vitae to transform; it\u2019s not cheap, which is why it's even more encouraging to make sure you make the most out of it.<br /> <br /> As for Humanity, I don\u2019t really consider it as part of the equation because my issue with the discipline simply stems from the fact that it ignores character abilities in a way that the vast majority of disciplines don\u2019t. (Possession is also like this, which i also don\u2019t like, but possession is another thing completly, and a discipline at 5 dots)<br /> <br /> All different character with access to the discipline also have to deal with humanity. If we take the sample characters; Marie also has less tools to resolve conflict, she might also have to take actions that will take a toll on her humanity, while Steven can afford to negotiate with a better chance of success. </div> </div> </div>So this is still a sticking point, because even if you aren't proposing that one should spend all their time shapeshifted into animals and don't think Humanity matters to Protean giving access to Physical Skills, the game rather disagrees with you on the latter and Vitae churn being often symptomatic of characters running hot in a way that seeking out a secure niche tends to be at odds with as far as holding a shape (literally or metaphorically).<br /> <br /> Humanity is how Requiem pushes characters who do a lot of things that they couldn't do if they were still human (and, prominently, would not be accepted as human by humans because this is a game where the vast majority of humans shy away from obvious supernatural displays and react adversely to being confronted with supernatural powers and violence). If you have a character who can only reliably sneak, fight, or hunt by turning into an animal, that starkly limits their ability to sneak, fight, or hunt in contexts where humans are around, and their rating in the trait that allows them to pass as a human will suffer accordingly, which is a problem on two fronts when playing as a socially-oriented member of the clan who gets worse at stopping themselves from going berserk the less humanlike they are.<br /> <br /> People tend to look past it because the gamelines' books tend to focus on the parts of being a person who is a monster that you can't infer from general familiarity with narratives about the mundane, but if you have to turn into a bear whenever you get into a fight, you're not fighting muggers or street toughs (or at least not anywhere people can see, spotty protection of the Lost Visage notwithstanding). The long-term gameplay loop of Requiem is that you get into situations that remind you you're not human anymore, become more monstrous to deal with the problems that exposes you to, get into situations that require you to shed more of the limitations of being like a human (and what is generally considered to be a good person), pursue more power to safeguard your ability to remain active, and so on.<br /> <br /> The more actively supernatural a character is, the more likely they are to be burning through their occult resources and/or drawing attention unless they specifically take steps to mitigate that \u2014 big explosive elder Devotions like Force of Nature are trump cards the old creatures make in the hopes that they don't have to use them instead of relying on the machines made of blood and magic and favors that they build around themselves to do the work for them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Is one character strictly better than the other, no of course not. Both have their respective strengths and weaknesses and both are valid. However, Steven certainly has access to more tools for the same investment, while Marie simply doubles down into making sure she has a plan B should her form not be possible to adopt.<br /> <br /> Steven can persuade, socialize, and is a pretty good investigator, while Marie\u2019s skills involve typical gangrel stuff. Steven also has firearms, so it\u2019s not like he can\u2019t fight at all either. I\u2019d say he\u2019s even a more versatile fighter; he simply has to be a bit more aware of his surroundings than Marie, but he's also better at it. </div> </div> </div>You have previously professed a concern about characters specializing in Weaponry needing to take dots in Brawl, and Firearms is not exempt from the rationale you have provided for that perspective in a game where the principal physical threats a character will encounter take reduced damage from conventional weapons in general and have a commonplace power that lets them dodge bullets, to say nothing of the constrains of Firearms as a Skill for deployment in the narrative.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> When they both transform, they invest resources to deal with a problem; all what Marie learned, all her previous experience, stops being relevant. Steven is as good as a fighter as her and I, personally, don\u2019t like that.<br /> <br /> In an ideal world, both character skills would still be a factor they shapeshift so they can show their individual strength instead of becoming a copy pasta of a particular animal. It actually does so for disciplines, so why are attribute skills also ignored? </div> </div> </div>Because Disciplines are supernatural qualities that animals will not typically have and the Disciplines for which this is relevant are the ones that make you more mobile, more durable, or more deadly, often in overtly unnatural ways. Beast's Skin letting you use your Disciplines is a rider on the fact that you're manifesting your Beast and its ability to wear the shapes of lesser beasts it's killed, and this is principally a narrative power allowing the character to be more at home in places where predatory/parasitic/plague-bearing animals dwell.<br /> <br /> Animals can be pretty badass fighters/hunters/sneaks in Chronicles, but Protean's core function is not to facilitate fighting/hunting/sneaking. If you hunt and eat a rat, you have the Attributes and Skills needed to be that rat, and your other Disciplines (or Devotions and/or Merits, if you wanna get fancy) are how to expand beyond that scope besides putting your Predatory/Unnatural Aspects on it to become a rat king.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Meaning, I can make both Steven and Marie into compelling characters with interesting stories and personalities. If I want to have fun with Protean, however, one is more incentivized than the other, and it\u2019s the clan outlier, which I find weird. I\u2019m not saying that\u2019s all that factors in, but there\u2019s certainly an incentive to do so. </div> </div> </div>Again, the incentive only exists if you exclusively look at the characters through the lens of optimizing their dicepools without regard for the contextual wrinkles of one character being able to engage in violence without becoming seven feet tall or go unseen without losing their opposable thumbs.<br /> <br /> You've directly recognized the fact that this approach is making excessive use of white-room scenarios. A character who cannot fight normal people without escalating to gun violence, frenzy, or overt Masquerade breaches is a character who cannot keep his place in the All Night Society for long. If you are playing a version of the game where the outlier is able to solve all his combat-related problems by turning into a bear and this doesn't have any impact on his ability to behave as a businessman, that is a consequence of the way the game is being run, not the way it is written.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> To me that would be like if Majesty 3 base power let you copy the social attributes of a mortal you drank. We\u2019d get fighters Daeva that would ignore their social skills and chase their local politicians, like Pok\u00e9mons. </div> </div> </div>The fact that that's not remotely within the idiom of Majesty in the first place makes this a very bad comparison \u2014 all the examples of using the stats from another creature involve possession or direct replication of the dead, and this is already a game where the Skill-stealing bodyjackers have an easier time using their host's Physical Skills than any others.<br /> <br /> If I were to take cues from later gamelines I might give vampires who rely on borrowed Skills a Condition that penalizes Traits they used without having them until they buy dots in them to close the gap, akin to Impostor from Demon or Echoes from Geist; I might also simply expand on the Blood Beats mechanic and allow characters to gain Beats toward raising a Trait possessed by a given vessel on the conclusion of a successful feeding scene, but that's admittedly an extra layer of bookkeeping.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> My idea of a well-written shapeshifting power would be to have it still allow Steven to shapeshift to become better at what he struggles in his base form while still respecting Marie for investing in her physical skills, respecting and respecting her clan stereotypes. </div> </div> </div>Marie's clan stereotypes include being good at the things Physical Skills do without having to turn into a wolf to do them. If she is better at tracking and hunting than a wolf, she does not need to turn into a wolf to hunt unless she needs some specific quality of a wolf, which the Discipline largely covers through Predatory Aspect instead of wholesale transformation.<br /> <br /> If she is better at fighting barehanded than a bear, she does not need to turn into a bear to fight unless she needs some specific quality of a bear, for which the Aspects, frenzy, other Disciplines and Merits are going to be a less context-dependent help.<br /> <br /> <br /> Chronicles in general makes Physical investment low-priority for characters who aren't expecting to need to engage with the world on a visceral, bodily, or violent level \u2014 if you're not needing to fight, run, hide, chase, sneak, forage, navigate, shoot, break and enter, or drive under pressure, you can put your Skill dots elsewhere and pick up the basics if you need to, whereas it's much more significant (albeit situational) for you to not have any training in how to fix an engine, build a wall, set a broken leg, read a chemical formula, interpret the law, delete the right folder, cut red tape, or perform a ritual, and while it'll be easier to muddle through lies, seduction, staredowns, public speaking, blending in, reading people, training pets, or putting the word out without training or talent, they're still more likely to be dramatically relevant to the average city-slicker who isn't living the fast life or working a job or pursuit that requires specialist knowledge.<br /> <br /> Add in the fact that travel into dreams and Astral realms only uses Physical Attributes if you're a) one of the Realest things in the inner realms or b) forgoing Dream Form mechanics entirely and it's easy to see the appeal in being able to take on alternate forms with better stats and further supplement them with other magical powers, but that doesn't <i>invalidate</i> a character who puts their character creation and advancement resources toward having good Physical Attributes and Skills that aren't locked behind being an animal suited to those things.<br /> <br /> <br /> With apologies for the lack of forwarded solutions, even without getting into the problems of &quot;debate mode&quot; being a clear and present risk for any thread that premises itself as a rant, none of us can really do much to help how you feel about an aspect of the game whose design principles run counter to your own without signing on for the bigger project of getting familiar with the needs of your already-houseruled game and making cases for why the RAW is the way it is. You do you.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-16T23:20:16-07:00","id":1561808,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Algarik</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1561664-protean-a-rant-and-some-homebrewed-solutions-looking-for-opinions?p=1561798#post1561798\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><i>Beast\u2019s skin ignore character physical stats and skill investment, I don\u2019t like this, it goes against some of my core personal design principle. </i></span></div> </div> </div> </div>Forum ate my post, follows &quot;short&quot; version:<br /> <br /> The above seemed secondary. Good to know it is the main problem. Simplify everything.<br /> <br /> Part 1 will be why this is good design, Part 2 will be suggestions. <hr /><br /> Part 1 - Protean is Good Design, bear with me.<br /> <br /> Fixed bonus is an optimization system. Makes who's good even better, while those who aren't have better things to do. It's a <i>gap enhancer</i>, even if mathematically the gap seems stable/proportionally reduced.<br /> <br /> Good for powers that increase effectiveness of character, but Physical Disciplines already do it better. Bad for powers that give options meant for everyone, because instead of new strategy for each, favors particular build.<br /> <br /> Example: Bear form gives Str +3, Brawl +1.<br /> <br /> - Character A Protean 3, Vigor 2, Str 4, Brawl 4.<br /> -- This character attacks with 10 dice, being their main Pool. As a bear increase to 14, being an absurd Pool for BP 1. It's also exceptionally strong for a bear in bear form.<br /> <br /> - Character B Protean 3, Auspex 2, Str 2, Brawl 0.<br /> -- This character attacks with 2 dice, not being deemed an option. As a bear increase to 6, with for a 2 Vitae expense remains a bad option. It's also a weak, puny bear.<br /> <br /> So, character A gets better at doing what they were already good at, which isn't the goal, and become an exceptional bear, which also isn't the goal. Character B doesn't derive any advantage besides becoming mediocre at something they don't focus on, and become an underwhelming bear.<br /> <br /> Protean goal is for the character to turn into an animal competently, no less, no more, not for use as bonus, but for use as animal. To be good for many different character types and not be just cheaper Vigor, it has to plain the field.<br /> <br /> Most Disciplines do that by requiring little to be useful. A 4 base dice pool (7 with Willpower) is enough to give you reliable access to the juicy effect. More dice make more reliable and all that, but 4 is good enough. Protean doesn't work like that, so this design won't work.<br /> <br /> What makes Protean still respect your stats, though? Wild bear vanishes behind wall, from there comes a person. Well, 1,80m tall Str 5 kung-fu master is distinct set of strategies from scrawny Str 1 lawyer. They will have very different uses for bear form, and one is evidently better at using animal disguise to ambush and beat the crap out. But both have useful bear.<br /> <br /> None will use bear for stats most of the time. And advantage of not needing it is big. <hr /><br /> Part 2 - Ideas:<br /> <br /> 1. Lock Vitae. Each 3 extra dots you get from form, -1 Vitae/turn while in it.<br /> <br /> 2. Dead Dice. Difference between your Pool and animal Pool is Marked Dice. If Animal Pool is bigger, Marked Dice lose 10-Again. If yours is bigger Marked Dice get 9-Again.<br /> <br /> 3. Willpower Special. Use the smaller Pool between you and animal. When you spend Willpower, you can use the bigger Pool until your next turn.<br /> <br /> 4. Animal Growth. If any trait of the animal is smaller than yours, you have to &quot;pay&quot; 2 Beats to transform. Those beats go towards buying the trait in question.<br /> <br /> 5. Slow Transformation. Even after paying the last Vitae to transform, you're incapacitated for 1 round for each dot you have to gain in animal form.<br /> <br /> 6. Bestial Pool. The dots the animal have above you are the Beast's Toll. Each scene you spend in animal form, the ST adds your Toll to your Bestial Pool. The ST spend dice in this Pool to give you penalties in Frenzy or bonuses in rolls you don't want to exceed yourself. They only go away being spent and can bring you to a chance die. Usually more successes = more control, but not those dice.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-17T05:29:26-07:00","id":1561818,"title":"","content":"My two cents, already a lot had been written, most of which I agree with.<br /> As Monteparnas said: Protean is not a combat discipline. Even though can become quite good at it given some devotion (swarm form, Claws of Unholy, Force of Nature). Protean is a survival powerful and versatile discipline.<br /> With 2 vitae allows you to fly, run with ceethah (or wolf) speed, sneak into small holes as a rat or snake.<br /> And yes, if your PC hadn't invest in strength and yet has some dot in vigor, he can still benefit from a default strgth of 6 + vigor and higher helthboxes. With JUST 2 vitae.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":7079,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","user":"Marcus","date":". 07-17-2026, 10:44 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-17T14:03:35-07:00","id":1561843,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:18px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Response to Satchel</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>On the Different Types of Kindred</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Hmm, I think I understand.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Sorry, I don\u2019t have much to add, unfortunately. Interesting stuff though.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>On Humanity</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I don\u2019t see Humanity as relevant, for that reason: my issue with Protean 3 is not an issue of power scaling. It\u2019s neither too strong nor too weak; it\u2019s ultimately simply that <i>it ignores a character's physical attributes and traits.</i></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">As for the inherent loss of Humanity incurred by using supernatural powers, I can see it from a Watsonian perspective; wearing your Beast as skin and using precious Vitae in a liberal manner makes you a terrible monster.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">However, I don\u2019t think the issue arise that much in actual play. Maybe I\u2019m reading the sample breaking points wrong, but:</span><ul><li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Injuring someone over blood is a breaking point at Humanity 7.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">One full week without human contact is a breaking point at Humanity 7.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Accidental killing and impassioned violence are breaking points at Humanity 4.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Riding the Wave of Frenzy is a breaking point at Humanity 8. (Too high in my opinion.)</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Urging another\u2019s behavior with a Discipline or depriving another of consent with a Discipline is a breaking point at Humanity 9 or 8 respectively. (Way too high in my opinion.)</span></li> </ul><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">One could make the argument that transforming into a predator will risk Humanity, but I don\u2019t see it as much worse than riding the Wave of Frenzy, which is pretty much practicing with your Beast.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I don\u2019t know how much Humanity factors into your typical chronicles, but in mine what usually happens is that player characters will hover around 5 if they are involved in Kindred politics, and violent characters will hover around 3 or 4 depending on the Banes selected and how much violence they use.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I will grant you one thing though: Gangrel absolutely got the worst possible curse out of all of clans. Being capped at Humanity for all frenzy tests is absolutely devastating.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Which, in my opinion, is another point for Steven; if you have access to more tools that aren\u2019t violent, you risk your Humanity less.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>On Firearms vs. Brawl</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Sure, Firearms isn\u2019t perfect. It\u2019s simply another skill set. If range combat is involved however he has that tool.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>On Beast\u2019s Skin Discipline</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I don\u2019t see a convincing reason why some transferable skills should not be taken into account. Assuming that the power doesn\u2019t take over your mind, I don\u2019t see why your knowledge of combat positioning, your ability to find shelter in the wild or ability to move around undented should factor in <i>at all.</i></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">While I certainly consider narrative in game design, I don\u2019t think it takes precedence over mechanics; I see them as an equally important piece of the equation. (I\u2019m not saying you don\u2019t also consider it. just for the record.)</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Any narrative justification can be made in support of or against the argument of allowing physical traits or not, and since it\u2019s blood magic, I don\u2019t think anyone can be wrong if they at least engage with the narrative aspect of the power.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I see the decision to lock out physical traits as a mechanical decision rather than a narrative one. I\u2019m not in the author\u2019s head, however, so I can only assume.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Cru\u00e0c has a ritual called <i>Donning the Beast\u2019s Flesh</i> that also allows an animal transformation. This one locks out all Disciplines except Auspex.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">The power states: <i>\u201cWhen any vampire dons the accessory, he can take the rough shape of an animal with the expenditure of a point of Vitae and three turns of transformation. The animal is a vague approximation, but imperfect in unnatural ways to a trained observer. Unlike the transformations granted by Protean, the vampire loses access to all his Disciplines, save for Auspex in the beast\u2019s shape.\u201d</i> (<i>Secrets of the Covenants</i>, p. 184)</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">No justification is provided in the power for why all Disciplines beside auspex are blocked. After all, one could make the narrative argument that Disciplines should actually be allowed but Attributes and Skills left untouched, as the transformation is an approximation, not a copy.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">However, it is not, because that\u2019s how the author wanted the power to work mechanically. I assume it\u2019s because they did not feel comfortable with the idea that a ritual could overshadow a clan Discipline power. I could be wrong though.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>On Optimizing: Steven vs. Marie</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Steven is simply more versatile than Marie. The incentive is that Steven can cover a lot more aspect of the all-night society. Having an easier time socializing will certainly make it easier to make use of social maneuvers.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Marie\u2019s better at the physical stuff most of the time: Athletics, Brawl, Stealth, and Survival.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Steven is better at the social game, all of the time.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">When they make use of Beast\u2019s Skin, they are equal.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I dislike that. I see it as an incentive to invest into skills that are not physical when I have a character with access to Protean. If I ever want to make a Gangrel character, it pushes me away from the clan stereotype.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">You might not see it that way; it\u2019s perfectly valid. I\u2019m not trying to debate your perception.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>On my Majesty Example</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">All comparisons will be flawed, and I\u2019ll admit I\u2019m generally bad at those. I\u2019ll try another one.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Awe boosts a Kindred\u2019s Presence; they gain Majesty dots to all Presence rolls.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Let\u2019s imagine that instead Awe would make it so a vampire\u2019s dice pool would be set to Majesty dots + 5 for all pools that would include Presence. Daeva would then have the incentive to ignore their Presence in favor of physical skills.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">What I attempted to express was that if your clan power pushes you away from your clan stereotype, then the outlier will start to show more than stereotypes, redefining the very stereotype.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Again, you might not agree that this is the case. I\u2019m not trying to convince you. I\u2019m trying to express how I perceive it.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>On the Rest of the Reply</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I don\u2019t disagree.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">As for homebrew, I\u2019m not asking for anyone to actually come up with solutions; that burden is on me.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Maybe one day I should actually post them on the forum if anyone\u2019s interested. I\u2019d have to format the whole thing to make it readable, however.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">It\u2019s super appreciated for those who do, however.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Response to Monteparnas</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Damned, sorry to hear your post got eaten. I write my replies in Word to avoid that specific problem. The log off time seems incredibly short on the forum.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>On Part 1</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I don\u2019t disagree. It\u2019s a design; it has its strengths and weaknesses. It just happens to be one I don\u2019t vibe with.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I think I can devise something that respects the fantasy of getting better at physical aspects by turning into animals, while still respecting previous character investment and their identities.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>On Part 2</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Thank you for proposing solutions! Appreciated, I\u2019ll go over them.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Ideas 1, 3, and 5 are straight nerfs to Protean. This is not my intended goal. I think we have a consensus: Protean 3 has inherent drawbacks. I don\u2019t think it needs to be made worse, especially when mental Disciplines are already so much more useful.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Idea 2 is interesting; it goes along with what Heavy Arms proposed. My issue would be having to calculate the number of marked dice each time. It would add unnecessary tracking. I've factored in the 9-again in my rework bellow.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Idea 4: I\u2019m confused on how it would work. You pay Beats if the animal is weaker than you? I Assume that you meant the reverse? You pay beats if your stats are under that of the animals. How would that work if that were the case? You\u2019d basically pay experience each time you\u2019d shift? Or you\u2019d need to hold out a reserve of beats that would be locked out for the duration of the transformation?</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Idea 6 has the merit of incorporating the Beast into the power. Also, it's kind of a nerf for most starting character. But it\u2019s an interesting idea, I don\u2019t hate it, but I\u2019m also not looking for a nerf.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I\u2019ll actually try to devise what I envision for the Discipline below, so I can actually discuss something I\u2019m looking forward to use.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Response to Marcus</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I agree. I\u2019m not saying Protean is a combat Discipline; it\u2019s a multi-purpose tool focused on physical adaptation. Combat just happens to be something it can be good at.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">But I have no issue with the fact that it\u2019s a good combat power.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:18px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Beast\u2019s Skin Rework</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><i>The human form is weak and unfit for a true predator. The Beast wants nothing else than to be let out of its cage. While it lurks in the dark corner of a vampire\u2019s mind, the Beast dreams of new predatory forms to perfect its hunts, taking inspiration from all those predators the vampire has previously consumed. A vampire can unleash those forms, warping their flesh, growing extra muscle, or folding in on themselves until they closely resemble the shape of an animal predator \u2014 or something truly wicked, should they listen to the Beast\u2019s every whim.</i></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Cost:</b> 2 Vitae<br /> <b>Dice Pool:</b> None<br /> <b>Action:</b> Instant<br /> <b>Duration:</b> Infinite</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">When activated, the vampire\u2019s flesh ripples and warps to take the form of an animal. They must have consumed the beast \u2014 killing it by feeding from it. They can only take the form of predatory animals, carrion-eaters, plague-carrying beasts, and parasites between Size 1 and 7. A vampire can keep a number of forms ready equal to their dots in Protean; to add or replace one, they must consume have consumed appropriate beast in the last lunar month, then slumber for a full day and night in their unmarked grave.<i> (Or in torpor if I end up changing Unmarked Grave, but let\u2019s ignore that for now.)</i></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Each form is built using the following rules:</span><ul><li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Move around up to Protean dots' worth of Attributes between Strength, Dexterity, and Stamina at a rate of 1for 1. Normal Attribute maximums still apply.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Add 2 extra dots to distribute Strength, Dexterity, Stamina, or Wits. Normal Attribute maximums still apply.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Add 2 + Protean dots to distribute among the following Skills: Athletics, Brawl, Intimidation, Stealth, and Survival. These Skills cannot be raised past 4 this way. If you already have at least 4 in a Skill, you can invest 2 dots to gain 9-again, or 4 dots to gain 8-again while using that Skill.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Set Size between 1 and 7. Size 6 inflicts a \u20131 penalty to Stealth. Size 7 inflicts a \u20132 Stealth penalty and increases the Strength limit to 6.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Gain any of the animal's normal methods of movement, using the animal's base Speed factor to determine your Speed.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Gain any of the animal's natural weapons.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Lower any physical Attribute of your choice to a minimum of 1.</span></li> </ul><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Once the form has been built, compare it to the animal's physical Attributes.</span><ul><li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">If any of the form's stats are lower than the animal's stats, the animal shape might look a bit frail, sickly, or sluggish based on the lower Attribute, but it is otherwise normal enough to pass as an animal.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">You gain the <b>Cryptid</b> Condition for as long as you remain in your form if any of the following statements are true:</span><ul><li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Before taking Vigor into account, your form's Strength is higher than the animal's Size.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Before taking physical Disciplines into account, your form has at least one physical Attribute that's 2 dots above the normal animal Attribute.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Before taking physical Disciplines into account, all your form's physical Attributes are above the normal animal Attributes.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">If the animal's size is different that the normal animal's size.</span></li> </ul></li> </ul><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Once transformed, a vampire is limited by their form's physiology, with all that entails. They may be unable to use some physical Merits at the Storyteller's discretion. If they lack the cryptid condition, they look like a normal animal of their species. Kindred still picks up on predatory aura, and without blush of life the animal shape remains undead to close inspection.</span><br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">The vampire can remain transformed indefinitely, but they retain their kindred nature; they can use any kindred ability and discipline as normal, and they still feel the need for blood burning within them. Finally, sunlight still harms them.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Cryptid</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">You let your Beast loose and it exaggerated its predatory desire in a way that renders your animal form unnerving and wrong. A sick mockery of the lifeform it's trying to imitate.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">A bear with too much Dexterity might look lanky and weird or have an extra pair of limbs. A dog with too much Strength might have a huge misshapen head with large fangs. A rat with too much Stamina might be bloated to the point that it looks like it's about to burst if you touch it.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">For as long as you suffer from this Condition, any character who looks at you knows there is something unnatural about you.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Possible Source:</b> Exceeding an animal's capabilities with Beast's Skin.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Resolution:</b> Transforming back into your normal shape.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Beat:</b> The character breaches the Masquerade in a way that creates a new cryptid legend. Be pursued for the sole purpose of proving your form is a real cryptid.</span><br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Applications</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Let's see how it would apply to Steven and Marie. I'll present 3 forms for each: a Battle Form, a Tracking Form, and a Stealth Form.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Marie's Battle Form \u2014 Bear Form</b></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Attributes:</b> Strength 6 (+2), Dexterity 1 (\u20131), Stamina 4/5 (+1, 5 with Resilience), Wits 3</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Skills:</b> Athletics 4 (+1), Brawl 4 (+2), Intimidation 3, Stealth 2 (\u2013), Survival 4 (+2)</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Initiative:</b> 5</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Defense:</b> 5</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Speed:</b> 13 (species factor 6)</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Size:</b> 4 (includes a 2-die penalty to Stealth)</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Health:</b> 11 (includes Resilience)</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Steven's Battle Form \u2014 Bear Form</b></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Attributes:</b> Strength 5 (+4), Dexterity 1 (\u20132), Stamina 2 (\u2013), Wits 3</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Skills:</b> Brawl 4, Intimidation 1</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Initiative:</b> 6</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Defense:</b> 1</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Speed:</b> 12 (species factor 6)</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Size:</b> 7 (includes \u20132 Stealth)</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Weapon Attacks:</b> Bite 9D (+2 DMG), Claws 9D (+1 DMG)</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Health:</b> 9</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Battle Form Analysis</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Both decided to take a bear form into battle, the strongest battle form they could think of.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Of course, Marie was always going to be better. She invested in her physical traits. Her bear form is actually pretty close to a bear's actual stats. She's more sluggish than one, but that's nothing she can't overcome with her base Skills.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Steven still isn't too shabby. His Defense is bad, and his bear looks like he's out of shape, but that's the price he pays for having ignored all those training sessions for most of his life. Still, don't let this fool you: his bear form will still rip you apart if you're not prepared.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">If he wanted a more balanced battle form, Steven could go for a dog and invest a bit less in Strength. Or actually start training his physical Skills.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Marie's Tracking Form \u2014 Large Dog</b></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Attributes:</b> Strength 4 (\u2013), Dexterity 3 (\u2013), Stamina 3/4 (\u2013, 4 with Resilience), Wits 4 (+1)</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Skills:</b> Athletics 4 (+1), Brawl 3 (+1), Intimidation 3, Stealth 2 (\u2013), Survival 4 (+1, with 9-again (+2))</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Initiative:</b> 5</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Defense:</b> 7</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Speed:</b> 14 (species factor 7)</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Size:</b> 4</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Weapon Attacks:</b> Bite 7D (+0 DMG)</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Health:</b> 8 (includes Resilience)</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Steven's Tracking Form \u2014 Raven</b></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Attributes:</b> Strength 1 (\u2013), Dexterity 4 (+1), Stamina 2 (\u2013), Wits 4 (+1)</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Skills:</b> Athletics 2 (+2), Intimidation 1, Survival 3 (+3)</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Initiative:</b> 6</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Defense:</b> 5</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Speed:</b> 15 (species factor 10)</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Size:</b> 2</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Weapon Attacks:</b> Beak 1D (+0 DMG)</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Health:</b> 5</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Tracking Form Analysis</b></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">As expected, Marie's dog form was always going to be stronger. This is a good hybrid form in which she benefits heavily from having already invested in what the form makes her better at doing. This is also not a bad shape for a more discreet battle form.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Steven opted for a raven. With its extra senses and flight, it would allow him to cover more ground. Additionally, because the raven is a less physically impressive animal, he could easily pull it off without looking like a scrawny animal; call it blending in in plain sight. Besides, Size 2 allows him to fit into smaller spaces.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Marie's Stealth Form \u2014 Cat</b></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Attributes:</b> Strength 2 (\u20132), Dexterity 5 (+3), Stamina 3/4 (\u2013, 4 with Resilience), Wits 4 (+1)</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Skills:</b> Athletics 4 (+1), Brawl 2, Intimidation 3, Stealth 4 (+2, with 9-again (+2)), Survival 2 (\u2013)</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Initiative:</b> 5</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Defense:</b> 8</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Speed:</b> 14 (species factor 7)</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Size:</b> 2</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Weapon Attacks:</b> Bite/Claw +4 (+0 DMG)</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Health:</b> 6 (includes Resilience)</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Steven's Stealth Form \u2014 Rat</b></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Attributes:</b> Strength 1 (\u2013), Dexterity 5 (+2), Stamina 2 (\u2013), Wits 3 (\u2013)</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Skills:</b> Athletics 2 (+3), Stealth 3 (+3), Intimidation 1</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Initiative:</b> 6</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Defense:</b> 5</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Speed:</b> 8 (species factor 2)</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Size:</b> 1</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Health:</b> 3</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Stealth Form Analysis</b></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Marie chose a cat because it allowed her to retain a bit more of her natural Strength without looking supernatural, while Steven decided to opt for a rat form since it was easier for him to pull off.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Both forms are pretty good at Stealth. Marie's better physical Skill set allows her to remain a bit more versatile in her form.<br /> <br /> <b><span style=\"font-size:14px\">Bonus form for fun</span></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:14px\"><b>John Gangrel's Messed-up looking Dog</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Attributes:</b> Strength 7/10 (\u2013, 10 with vigor), Dexterity 5 (+1), Stamina 6/11 (-, 11 with Resilience), Wits 5 (+1)</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Skills:</b> Athletics 4 (9 again(+2)), Brawl 6(9 again(+2)), Intimidation 4, Stealth 4 (9 again,+2), Survival 5 (\u2013)</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Initiative:</b> 9</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Defense:</b> 11 (include celerity 2)</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Speed:</b> 21 (species factor 7)</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Size:</b> 4 </span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Weapon Attacks:</b> Bite +16 (+0 DMG)</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Health:</b> 15 (includes Resilience)</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>John Analysis</b></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Well, this thing is the stuff of nightmares. With a huge, disgusting head, bloated red eyes, it moves so fast it's blurry, and, oh boy, it's so jacked you can see its muscles tear at the joints.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Rumors about a Hellhound circulate among the elderly folks of the nearby village. They tell tales of the Hellhound to their grandkids from that time they went hunting. Of course, it's ridiculous. No one believes that. Probably better that way, as there's nothing they could do to stop it anyway. Better to sleep soundly than stress about something you have no control over...</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">It's also something John, a Gangrel, would whip out as a last resort. Still, investing his 2 Vitae, which he can only regain from fellow Kindred, should yield results.</span><br /> \u200b<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Conclusion</b></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I originally made Steven to be the worst possible physical character; he has no dots in any significant animal Skill set. Marie benefits the most from respecting her clan Stereotype and John Gangrel gets to become the stuff of legends.<br /> <br /> Yet Steven still gains significant benefits from transforming, just like an asocial Daeva would still benefit from Awe if they suddenly needed to become likable, or a soft-spoken Nosferatu would benefit from Nightmare if they needed to scare someone off. And that's even without mentioning the added benefit of looking like an animal.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I think the cryptid condition might actually be pretty fun, it allows a character that really want to go ham to burst through the normal animal limit at the risk of making the form very obvious. It makes finding a bear or tiger less mandatory for an optimized battle form, but still interesting if a bear would be appropriate for the region.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">The Cryptid Condition giving Beats is entirely optional. I just thought it would be a fun way to add to cryptid legends and emphasize the dangerousness of looking like a weird, messed-up animal.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I think my proposition respects character Attribute and Skill investment, doesn't ignore the drawbacks of Protean, and isn't a straight nerf or a buff.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Feel free to give your opinion or constructive propositions on my proposed solution, but let's keep in mind that my intended goal is to include the character's Attributes as part of the equation when transformed.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I'm not interested in debating whether my design philosophy is good or bad on its own.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I see the virtues of both, and my preference falls on one side of the debate. I'm not interested in arguing over preference. While I truly appreciate all your feedback, I already spent more time and effort writing this than some of my bachelor's degree philosophy papers. With respect, I want to put my energy toward more productive uses.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Thanks again to all.</span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-17T15:44:48-07:00","id":1561853,"title":"","content":"Making a (relatively) brief case for Humanity as a concern because this is one of those things about which the game is a little roundabout (the other being &quot;the Predatory Aura being free to use on mortals and applying Conditions and bonuses that make it easier for you to get exceptional successes on certain types of behavior effectively counterbalances vampires not regaining Willpower with rest by giving them the Bestial Triad as a loose extra Vice by making it easier for you to gain the Inspired Condition from acting like a monster&quot;):<br /> <br /> Riding the wave is not just &quot;practicing with the Beast.&quot; Regardless of how good your Resolve + Composure pool is, any failed roll on the set of rolls counts as a dramatic failure. Dramatic failure on a roll to resist frenzy means the frenzy cannot end until the character reaches a breaking point, which is going to be a more severe breaking point the lower the character's Humanity is. Messing up at riding the wave <i>forces the issue</i> re: vampires' ability to be nonviolent.<br /> <br /> Any of the things on the sidebar of suggested modifiers for frenzy could potentially trigger a check for frenzy \u2014 a list that includes things like being injured, insulted, or hungry \u2014 and without powers and Merits to expand its scope the only way to ride the wave is to encounter a frenzy trigger and spend Willpower. Gangrel capping their rolls to resist frenzy (and specifically not their rolls to ride the wave) means they're less likely to exceptionally succeed at normal rolls to resist frenzy (and thus disincentivized to spend Willpower to hold it off and get a big bonus to the singular roll to resist because they're less likely to get it back all at once) and also less likely to normally succeed (thus minimizing the degree to which they have a mounting penalty from the Tempted Condition that they need to fall to frenzy, kill someone, or have a meaningful connection with a Touchstone to clear), and so their Clan Bane has them encouraged to respond to frenzy triggers by gambling on their ability to steer the Beast in a way that makes them more likely to kill someone the lower their Humanity is.<br /> <br /> At the best of times, a Gangrel who e.g. unexpectedly has their outfit ruined or gets too close to a bloody wound has a reduced capacity to keep it together, and if they attempt to get the Beast to do something other than what it wants then they run the risk of escalating to murder \u2014 impassioned violence being a Humanity 4 breaking point means that's a concern until you drop to Humanity 3, and likewise with impassioned killing until Humanity 2, and removing those breaking points with banes doesn't make this problem go away (and in some ways makes it worse).<br /> <br /> Frenzy triggers being strictly in the ST's hands as to when a given event counts means this is technically up in the air, as does any given table or character's decision on what turning into or spending time in a nonhuman shape does to a character's Humanity beyond the sample breaking points, but it's fairly observable that the mechanics of the game are built for vampires to have uncontrolled violence as a regular risk to manage and frenzy tilting characters toward being more effective at violence is the main pressure valve for Humanity, to the point where the Condition for dramatically failing a Detachment check shares a resolution criterion with the Condition for successfully bottling up the Beast.<br /> <br /> The Gangrel's bane forces them to contend with this more often and the classical way in which the clan mitigates the issue is to retreat from territories and circumstances in which they are more likely to encounter triggers for frenzy \u2014 hard to get insulted by a stranger or encounter open wounds or large flames if you spend most of your time miles away from human activity, and keeping up appearances every couple of weeks is relatively simple.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-17T18:14:29-07:00","id":1561861,"title":"","content":"Explaining Idea 4:<br /> <br /> If the Animal Form has an Attribute or Skill above your values, you &quot;pay&quot; 2 beats.<br /> <br /> This is not about total, it's for any trait you don't have yourself at that level. So if it is weaker in almost everything, but has more Stealth, you &quot;pay&quot; 2 beats.<br /> <br /> Your payment is not a loss. It actually goes to one of the traits in question. In that case, to stealth.<br /> <br /> Basically Beast's Skin makes you get closer to the animal: every 5 beats locked in the trait you have 1 Experience, with enough Experiences you buy the trait.<br /> <br /> So the actual price is the commitment to raise that trait until it get to be the same. The animal still benefits you with Size, senses, movement, etc. <hr /><br /> Like your proposal. Won't use because find it too much bookkeeping for my group, but very good.<br /> <br /> I'll possibly use it as inspiration for a Devotion, and loved the Cryptid Condition. <hr />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":5826,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","user":"monteparnas","date":". 07-17-2026, 09:17 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-18T09:27:42-07:00","id":1561896,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Response to Stachel.</b></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Oops, sorry about the typo, I mean to write \u2018\u2019pactising\u2019\u2019 with the beast, which is a deformation of the French verb ''pactiser'' which should have translated into ''making a pact with'' as it appears you can't make a verb with the word pact in English.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">What I was getting at was that riding the wave, which I see as striking a deal with your beast by essentially choosing its intent to wield its power as you see fit was a breaking point I would have categorized as lower on the chart, but I guess the risk is incredibly high that you mess up.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">However, I\u2019d add that frenzy does not necessarily imply killing someone. I\u2019d argue that punishing someone who insulted you can be done in many ways, shouting and lashing out are valid in my book. Unless you dramatically fail a frenzy test, it doesn\u2019t have to reach a breaking point.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Anyway, I\u2019ll have to disengage from the humanity side of the discussion, it\u2019s interesting but it goes beyond what I originally indented for this thread and I lack the time to invest into doing it justice.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Thanks for your input, though.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Response Monteparnas</b></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Oooh, I see. Idea 4 is very interesting. Probably my favorite of the bunch. It attacks the problem I had of having gangrels being incentivized to avoid physical skill from a completely different angle ; If you spend time doing physical stuff, it stands to reason that it\u2019s those skills that would rise up rather than your mental or social skills.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I\u2019m not going to use it as I use a different experience system, since I don\u2019t like flat XP for all traits, but it\u2019s certainly a cool idea.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I\u2019m also glad to see that you like my proposal!</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I personally don\u2019t mind the bookkeeping too much, as I ask gangrel players to have all their forms ready on hand since it requires double checking if their animal forms are influenced by their mental stats, such as defense being based on Wits, or if any physical discipline alter their speed, attack and defense, etc. It takes a little bit longer to build, but it\u2019s nothing more than a 3-minute exercise at worse.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Do let me see what you design as devotion if you feel like it, I\u2019d be curious to see what you cook. </span><span style=\"font-family:Segoe UI Emoji\">\ud83d\ude0a</span><br /> <br /> \u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-18T11:51:53-07:00","id":1561903,"title":"","content":"As a side note since I'm trying to avoid repeating points others are making:<br /> <br /> Pact is already a verb in English. &quot;I pacted with a demon,&quot; is a grammatically correct sentence. The verb form has largely fallen out of use though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-19T07:51:44-07:00","id":1561930,"title":"","content":"Ah yes, it appears you are right, thanks!<br /> <br /> To my knowledge, the French verb isn't considered archaic, but it's usually considered a bit more ''negative'' or as in striking a deal with something evil or with an enemy such as :<br /> <br /> - ''Pactiser avec le Diable''. (Making a pact with the Devil) <br /> - ''Pactiser avec l'ennemi '' Which is often used to denounce a form of treason. ''Traitre! Vous avez pactiser avec l'ennemi! '' Would mean ''Traitor! You were striking deals with the enemy!''<br /> <br /> It isn't really used to refer to legal pact anymore. <br /> <br /> Don't quote me on that however, that's just my lived experienced nothing scholarly about my analysis.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-19T13:38:47-07:00","id":1561933,"title":"","content":"Either way, Riding the Wave is not that.<br /> <br /> The point of the Beast isn't goal, it's lack of control. The idea is to be violent and direct, without care. Riding the Wave just let you point vague directions, like choosing a target rather than attacking the nearest person, but the attack is still using extreme violence.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-20T07:23:45-07:00","id":1561970,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I get that, what I meant by striking a deal wasn\u2019t a rationally negotiated deal. It\u2019s more like steering your beast, but it still requires you to \u2018\u2019deal\u2019\u2019 with it. In the sense that you are actively trying to steer its intentions.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">My confusion stems from why is it so high on the chart and not a bit lower? Humanity 8, to me, seems very high. But I also think that a frenzy on its own should trigger a humanity test for humane kindred.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I also scratch my head at the fact that creating a ghoul and depriving another of consent with a discipline is also a breaking point at 8. Those are two very supernatural things you can do; must worse than spending a week active without human contact, which some humans also do.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">The beast does feed on isolation, though I\u2019m aware, so maybe speeding a week alone with your beast is more damaging , idk.</span><br /> <hr /><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I\u2019ll admit I reworked how humanity worked in my game, it\u2019s both equally more punishing at places and more lenient at others. Basically, humanity is divided into 8 categories (not my idea, i took inspiration from a forum post);</span><ul><li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Alienation :</b> Represents the beast feeding on the alienation most kindred feel when they are confronted with the fact that they are no longer human. Examples of breaking point include : watching a human eat a meal(10), falling into torpor(5), learning the fifth dot of a discipline(1), etc.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Beast :</b> Represents giving attention to the beast, or losing control in face of it. Breaking points include : Making more than one rouse check per night(10), entering frenzy(7), reaching blood potency 3(4), reaching any BP over 5(1), etc.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Domination :</b> The Beast feeds on feelings of superiority and control. When you use your beast to control others, you also open yourself to being controlled by your beast. Breaking points include : bypassing an animal\u2019s consent(9), creating a vinculum(7), creating a ghoul(4), creating a full vinculum(3), causing a breaking point with a discipline(2), etc.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Grief :</b> The beasts feed on feelings of loss and despair. Breaking points include : Death of an acquaintance(9), Losing your haven (variable), Death of a sire or childe (5), and Death of a touchstone(1), etc.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Isolation:</b> The beast feeds on isolation. When humans aren\u2019t there to distract you from your monstrous nature, the beast whispers grows stronger. Breaking points include : Rejected by a human(8), one week active without human contact(7), one month without human contact(3), rejected by a touchstone(1), etc.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Murder : </b>If you adopt the mindset of a killer, you adopt the mindset of the beast. The beast wants you to kill and hunt. Murder breaking point represents killing with intent. Breaking points include : plotting a murder without carrying it out(6), helping a murder(4), Premiditated killing(2), mass killing(1), etc.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Requiem :</b> Requiem represents the tendency kindred society has to suck the soul out of everyone in the all night society. Requiem breaking points are triggered by taking active part in kindred politics. Breaking points include : lying in defence of the masquerade(10), claiming a boon(8), thinking of humans like cattle(7), gaining the 4th dot of status in a covenant(4), striking a deal with the Strix without being coerced into it(1), etc.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\"><b>Violence :</b> If you succumb to the beast\u2019s violent instinct, it will grow stronger. Breaking points include : Feeding from the Unwilling(9), participating in a fight(7), injuring someone over blood(6), accidental killing (4), impassioned killing(3), torture(2), gruesome torture(1), etc.</span></li> </ul><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I also take into account the vampire current humanity for the purpose of breaking points. </span><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Basically, when a breaking point is reached, the player rolls 5 dice -1 per step below their humanity + their touchstone extra dice. </span><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">So, for example, a humanity 5 vampire increases a discipline to the fourth dot and they have a touchstone; They would roll 6 dice. (5-1, +2 extra dice from having a touchstone).</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Banes are applied to a full category, so naturally, violence, domination and murder are going to be a bit more popular to protect one\u2019s humanity, but I\u2019ve had players have lost humanity from angles that they weren\u2019t necessarily expecting before, like blood bounds and since they are very active in kindred politics, they\u2019ve been making a few tests now and then and it slowly chips away.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">Additionaly, while i\u2019m not sure it would work that way, i treat bane as if they completely removed that ability for a kindred to interact with that type of breaking point, meaning a dramatic failure on a frenzy roll means any violence act won\u2019t stop your frenzy. It can take a long while for a frenzied kindred with a bane on violence to calm down.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I don\u2019t play with beats (I award willpower instead when a beat would be gained) and I\u2019ve made humanity be worth a bit more experience, which I apply sanctity of merit to. When a kindred loses humanity, they can spend the refunded experience on vampire stuff, such as discipline, attributes and skills, as long as it drives them toward being a better predator.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">That way, rising back up is harder, because they have to actually regain that experience from other means. I also ask my players to wait for a number of months equal to BP before they can buy a dot of humanity, a span of time during which they must have taken part in at least one humanizing activity during each month and during which they haven\u2019t lost any extra humanity.</span><br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-20T21:18:57-07:00","id":1561986,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Algarik</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1561664-protean-a-rant-and-some-homebrewed-solutions-looking-for-opinions?p=1561970#post1561970\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">I get that, what I meant by striking a deal wasn\u2019t a rationally negotiated deal. It\u2019s more like steering your beast, but it still requires you to \u2018\u2019deal\u2019\u2019 with it. In the sense that you are actively trying to steer its intentions.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Aptos\">My confusion stems from why is it so high on the chart and not a bit lower? Humanity 8, to me, seems very high. But I also think that a frenzy on its own should trigger a humanity test for humane kindred.</span></div> </div> </div> </div>It's just to explain why the Frenzy goes until a Breaking Point: since you can't negotiate the Beast out of extreme violence, you can't stop it from doing something you'd rather not.<br /> <br /> As for why so high and why Frenzy isn't in it: aside any philosophical debate, it's a game need. Starting Humanity must be able to accommodate basic gameplay, which includes the use of all your abilities. For most tables giving Vampires the power to make Ghouls and take Dominate at chargen, just to punish them if they use those is extremely lame.<br /> <br /> Frenzy isn't a Breaking Point because it will generate Breaking Points, that will make Frenzy way more punitive and diminish the incentive to risk it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-09-21T23:01:39-07:00","id":1564264,"title":"","content":"In regards to your point about gaining the animal\u2019s skills You could simply remove that part or cap it at one less than the animal has. In regards to your argument that this is the only Disciple that bypasses a skill Obfuscate completely bypasses stealth not only for yourself but it even allows you to grant your entire party the same ability.<br /> There\u2019s also the fact that you need to drain a bear of blood before you can turn into it and by a strict reading of the rules spend a day in haven of soil so unless you have access to a wilderness where bears can be found you are taking the form of that bear that died at the local zoo right down to that distinctive patch of fur and that bear didn\u2019t get into any serious fights so probably wouldn\u2019t have great brawl skills.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4714-live-bait","name":"Live Bait","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"703"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-10-20T10:21:20-07:00","id":1564872,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">A bit late for the reply Live Bait. Sorry, I got busy.<br /> <br /> As for animal skills, I think I'll use the write-up I made for Beast's Shape in the future. I quite like it and so far I have made gangrel forms more unique to each of their respective users. My gangrel player switched to a Daeva, and currently no player owns protean, so I won\u2019t shy away from applying the change for future characters.<br /> <br /> For obfuscate, it\u2019s not false, but also not completely true. It\u2019s super strong, but a character still benefits from stealth.It\u2019s not as perfect as one would think.<br /> <br /> It\u2019s very strong in people, but we\u2019ve had quite a few stealth missions in which obfuscate was a useful tool but not fool proof. If a building you\u2019re infiltrating has cameras, you have to deal with those, as you\u2019ll leave behind video recordings of all your incriminating actions. Also, if you treat a mission as a collection of scenes, cloak of night is going to burn through any kindred\u2019s vitae, especially if they bring their friends along, which they can only partially obfuscate unless they have the devotion which allows them to (cloak of gathering IIRC), which is also very expensive.<br /> <br /> Finally, when launching a surprise attack with obfuscate 3, a character still need a bit of stealth or they risk simply failing.<br /> <br /> That being said, all disciplines have their strengths and they can quite often be used to sideline any part of the game with the right application. I\u2019m getting better at designing scenarios in which they are a tool and not the solution.<br /> <br /> </span></span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26771-algarik","name":"Algarik","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26771_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"115"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}